/* Study guide: 16 topics as HTML strings. */
const STUDY = [{"id": "sm-found", "sec": "SM", "title": "Formulae and Foundations", "r1": "Formulae and Foundations", "min": 25, "html": "\n<p class=\"lead\">Every spreadsheet question in Round 1 is built from the same few ideas: a cell holds either a value or a formula, a formula points at other cells, and copying a formula moves those pointers. Master these and the rest of the section is vocabulary.<\/p>\n\n<h3>1. What a spreadsheet actually is<\/h3>\n<p>A spreadsheet is a grid. Each box is a <b>cell<\/b>, named by its column letter and row number: <code>B3<\/code> is column B, row 3. A group of cells is a <b>range<\/b>, written with a colon: <code>B2:B6<\/code> is five cells in a column; <code>B2:D4<\/code> is a 3 × 3 block.<\/p>\n<p>A cell holds one of two things:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>a <b>value<\/b> you typed (a number like <code>500<\/code>, text like <code>North<\/code>, a date, TRUE or FALSE), or<\/li>\n<li>a <b>formula<\/b>, which always starts with <code>=<\/code>. The cell then <em>shows<\/em> the result but <em>holds<\/em> the recipe.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<div class=\"why\"><b>The core idea.<\/b> A formula is a recipe that points at other cells instead of containing numbers. If <code>C2<\/code> holds <code>=A2*B2<\/code>, then changing A2 changes C2 automatically. That is why a model built with references can answer \"what if the price were ₹90?\" in one keystroke, and a model with typed numbers cannot.<\/div>\n\n<h3>2. The four kinds of data<\/h3>\n<table>\n<tr><th>Type<\/th><th>Example<\/th><th>What to remember<\/th><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Number<\/td><td>1250, 0.08, -40<\/td><td>Right-aligned by default. A cell formatted as % holds a fraction: 8% is stored as 0.08.<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Text<\/td><td>\"Pen\", \"P104\"<\/td><td>Left-aligned. Text that looks like a number (\"500\" imported from a file) is ignored by SUM. Fix with <code>VALUE()<\/code> or multiply by 1.<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Boolean<\/td><td>TRUE, FALSE<\/td><td>Result of a comparison such as <code>=B2&gt;100<\/code>. In arithmetic TRUE counts as 1 and FALSE as 0, which powers many tricks.<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Date<\/td><td>14-Oct-2026<\/td><td>A date is a serial number (days since a start date), formatted to look like a date. So <code>=B2-B1<\/code> is the number of days between two dates.<\/td><\/tr>\n<\/table>\n\n<h3>3. How a formula is calculated<\/h3>\n<p>Excel and Google Sheets evaluate in a fixed order. Anything in brackets goes first, then powers (<code>^<\/code>), then multiplication and division (left to right), then addition and subtraction (left to right).<\/p>\n<div class=\"fx\">=100+20*2 &rarr; 140 &nbsp;|&nbsp; =(100+20)*2 &rarr; 240 &nbsp;|&nbsp; =2^3*2 &rarr; 16 &nbsp;|&nbsp; =100*(1+8%)^3 &rarr; 125.97<\/div>\n<p>When in doubt, add brackets. Brackets cost nothing and remove ambiguity. In particular, write growth as <code>(1+rate)^years<\/code>, never <code>1+rate^years<\/code>.<\/p>\n\n<h3>4. Cell references: the most tested idea<\/h3>\n<p>When you copy a formula, every reference inside it shifts by the same distance you moved. That is called a <b>relative<\/b> reference, and it is what lets one formula fill a whole column. When you do <em>not<\/em> want a reference to move (a tax rate, an interest rate, a price), you lock it with <code>$<\/code>.<\/p>\n<table>\n<tr><th>Written<\/th><th>Name<\/th><th>Copied down<\/th><th>Copied right<\/th><\/tr>\n<tr><td><code>B2<\/code><\/td><td>relative<\/td><td>B3, B4...<\/td><td>C2, D2...<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td><code>$B$2<\/code><\/td><td>absolute<\/td><td>stays $B$2<\/td><td>stays $B$2<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td><code>$B2<\/code><\/td><td>column locked<\/td><td>$B3<\/td><td>stays $B2<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td><code>B$2<\/code><\/td><td>row locked<\/td><td>stays B$2<\/td><td>C$2<\/td><\/tr>\n<\/table>\n<div class=\"eg\"><b>Worked example: a tax-rate table.<\/b> Prices run down column A (A2:A5). Tax rates run across row 1 (B1:D1). You want a table of price × (1 + rate) in B2:D5.\n<div class=\"fx\">B2: =$A2*(1+B$1)<\/div>\nWhy: as you copy <em>right<\/em>, you want the rate to move across B1, C1, D1 but the price to stay in column A, so lock the column of the price ($A). As you copy <em>down<\/em>, you want the price to move to A3, A4... but the rate to stay in row 1, so lock the row of the rate (B$1). Press F4 to cycle through the four forms.<\/div>\n<div class=\"trap\"><b>Watch out.<\/b> A formula that is right in row 2 and wrong from row 3 down almost always has a missing <code>$<\/code>. When you see a rate or constant being used by many rows, lock it.<\/div>\n\n<h3>5. Anatomy of a function<\/h3>\n<p>A function is a built-in recipe: a name, brackets, and arguments separated by commas. <code>SUM(B2:B6)<\/code> has one argument. <code>IF(B2&gt;100,\"High\",\"Low\")<\/code> has three. Arguments can be cells, ranges, numbers, text in quotes, or other functions (nesting). Read nested formulas from the inside out.<\/p>\n\n<h3>6. Logic: IF, AND, OR<\/h3>\n<div class=\"fx\">=IF(test, value_if_true, value_if_false)<\/div>\n<p>The test is any comparison: <code>=<\/code>, <code>&lt;&gt;<\/code> (not equal), <code>&gt;<\/code>, <code>&gt;=<\/code>, <code>&lt;<\/code>, <code>&lt;=<\/code>. Combine tests with <code>AND(t1,t2)<\/code> (all true), <code>OR(t1,t2)<\/code> (any true), <code>NOT(t)<\/code>.<\/p>\n<p><b>Tiers<\/b> are nested IFs. Test from the most demanding tier down, because IF stops at the first true test:<\/p>\n<div class=\"fx\">=IF(B1&gt;=1000000, B1*3%, IF(B1&gt;=500000, B1*2%, 0))<\/div>\n<p>If you tested <code>&gt;=500000<\/code> first, a sale of ₹12 lakh would stop there and pay 2%. Modern Sheets also offers <code>IFS(t1,v1,t2,v2,TRUE,default)<\/code>.<\/p>\n\n<h3>7. Summing, counting and averaging under conditions<\/h3>\n<table>\n<tr><th>Task<\/th><th>Function<\/th><th>Pattern<\/th><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Plain total<\/td><td>SUM<\/td><td><code>=SUM(B2:B50)<\/code><\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Count numbers / non-blanks<\/td><td>COUNT / COUNTA<\/td><td><code>=COUNTA(A2:A50)<\/code><\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Total where one condition holds<\/td><td>SUMIF<\/td><td><code>=SUMIF(range, criterion, sum_range)<\/code><\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Total where several hold<\/td><td>SUMIFS<\/td><td><code>=SUMIFS(sum_range, range1, crit1, range2, crit2)<\/code><\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Count with conditions<\/td><td>COUNTIF(S)<\/td><td><code>=COUNTIFS(A:A,\"South\",C:C,\"&gt;500\")<\/code><\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Average with conditions<\/td><td>AVERAGEIF(S)<\/td><td><code>=AVERAGEIF(A:A,\"North\",C:C)<\/code><\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Weighted total or average<\/td><td>SUMPRODUCT<\/td><td><code>=SUMPRODUCT(weights, scores)<\/code><\/td><\/tr>\n<\/table>\n<p>Criteria are written as text: <code>\"North\"<\/code>, <code>\"&gt;=100\"<\/code>, <code>\"&lt;&gt;0\"<\/code>. To compare with a cell, join with <code>&amp;<\/code>: <code>\"&gt;\"&amp;E2<\/code>. Note the order trap: SUMIF puts the range to add <em>last<\/em>, SUMIFS puts it <em>first<\/em>.<\/p>\n<div class=\"eg\"><b>SUMPRODUCT in plain words.<\/b> It multiplies two ranges element by element and adds the results. With weights 0.4, 0.35, 0.25 and scores 82, 74, 90 it computes 0.4×82 + 0.35×74 + 0.25×90 = 81.2. It is the cleanest way to get a total of \"quantity × price\" without a helper column.<\/div>\n\n<h3>8. Rounding<\/h3>\n<table>\n<tr><th>Function<\/th><th>Rule<\/th><th>2.5 gives<\/th><\/tr>\n<tr><td>ROUND(x,0)<\/td><td>nearest<\/td><td>3<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>ROUNDUP(x,0)<\/td><td>always away from zero<\/td><td>3<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>ROUNDDOWN(x,0)<\/td><td>always toward zero<\/td><td>2<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>CEILING / FLOOR<\/td><td>to a multiple<\/td><td>-<\/td><\/tr>\n<\/table>\n<p>The business rule decides the function. You cannot run 12.3 trucks, so use ROUNDUP. You cannot sell 0.7 of a ticket to break even, so round break-even <em>up<\/em>. Round only at the last step; rounding in the middle of a chain compounds error.<\/p>\n\n<h3>9. Text and dates in one page<\/h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Join text with <code>&amp;<\/code>: <code>=A2&amp;\" - \"&amp;B2<\/code>. Pull pieces with <code>LEFT<\/code>, <code>RIGHT<\/code>, <code>MID<\/code>. Clean with <code>TRIM<\/code> (removes stray spaces that break lookups). Format a number inside text with <code>TEXT(B2,\"0.0%\")<\/code>.<\/li>\n<li>Dates: <code>EDATE(date, 6)<\/code> is six calendar months later; <code>EOMONTH(date,0)<\/code> is the month end; <code>DATEDIF(start,end,\"M\")<\/code> is whole months; <code>YEARFRAC(start,end)<\/code> is years as a decimal; <code>TODAY()<\/code> updates daily.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n\n<h3>10. Reading errors<\/h3>\n<table>\n<tr><th>Error<\/th><th>Meaning<\/th><th>Usual cause<\/th><\/tr>\n<tr><td>#DIV/0!<\/td><td>divided by zero or blank<\/td><td>denominator cell is empty<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>#N/A<\/td><td>value not found<\/td><td>lookup key missing or has a trailing space<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>#VALUE!<\/td><td>wrong data type<\/td><td>text where a number is needed<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>#REF!<\/td><td>reference no longer exists<\/td><td>a referenced row or column was deleted<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>#NAME?<\/td><td>unknown name<\/td><td>misspelt function, or text without quotes<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>#NUM!<\/td><td>impossible number<\/td><td>IRR with no sign change, negative square root<\/td><\/tr>\n<\/table>\n\n<h3>Common traps<\/h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Typing a number inside a formula (<code>=B2*0.25<\/code>) so the model stops updating.<\/li>\n<li>Using ROUND where the situation needs ROUNDUP.<\/li>\n<li>Reversing SUMIF and SUMIFS argument order.<\/li>\n<li>Percent confusion: typing 8 into a % cell gives 800%. Type 8% or 0.08.<\/li>\n<li>Forgetting that a lookup or sum range was not locked and slid off the table when copied.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<div class=\"try\"><b>Try it in a sheet (5 minutes).<\/b> Put prices in A2:A5 and rates 5%, 12%, 18% in B1:D1. Build the full price-with-tax grid with one formula. Then change one price and watch the row update. If you needed to edit more than one cell, a reference is not locked correctly.<\/div>\n"}, {"id": "sm-look", "sec": "SM", "title": "Lookups and data joins", "r1": "Lookups and data joins", "min": 25, "html": "\n<p class=\"lead\">A lookup answers \"given this key, what is the matching value?\" A join is the same idea applied to a whole column. Most real data lives in separate tables (prices in one, orders in another), so this is how you connect them.<\/p>\n\n<h3>1. The idea: key and table<\/h3>\n<p>Picture a price list: each row has a <b>key<\/b> that identifies it (product code) and some <b>attributes<\/b> (name, price). A lookup takes a key you already have and returns one attribute from the matching row.<\/p>\n<div class=\"why\"><b>Why not just type the price?<\/b> Because the price list will change, and because you may have 5,000 orders. A lookup keeps one source of truth: change the price once and every order updates.<\/div>\n\n<h3>2. VLOOKUP: the classic<\/h3>\n<div class=\"fx\">=VLOOKUP(key, table, column_number, exact_or_approx)<\/div>\n<p>Read it as: \"go down the <em>first column<\/em> of the table to find the key, then move across to column number N and return that cell.\" Always set the last argument to <code>FALSE<\/code> (or <code>0<\/code>) for an exact match.<\/p>\n<div class=\"eg\">Table in A2:C6 with Code, Item, Price. Key in E1 = P104.\n<div class=\"fx\">=VLOOKUP(E1, A2:C6, 3, FALSE) &rarr; 25<\/div>\nColumn 3 of the table is Price.<\/div>\n<p><b>Limits of VLOOKUP:<\/b> the key must be in the table's first column; the answer must be to its right; and the column number is typed, so inserting a column silently breaks it.<\/p>\n\n<h3>3. INDEX + MATCH: the flexible pair<\/h3>\n<p>Split the job in two. <code>MATCH<\/code> finds <em>where<\/em> the key sits. <code>INDEX<\/code> picks the item at that position from any column you choose.<\/p>\n<div class=\"fx\">=MATCH(key, lookup_range, 0) &rarr; a position number<br>=INDEX(return_range, position) &rarr; the item at that position<br>=INDEX(return_range, MATCH(key, lookup_range, 0))<\/div>\n<div class=\"eg\">Names in A2:A6, IDs in B2:B6. Find the name for ID 7101 (the name is to the <em>left<\/em> of the ID, so VLOOKUP fails).\n<div class=\"fx\">=INDEX(A2:A6, MATCH(7101, B2:B6, 0)) &rarr; Meera<\/div>\nMATCH says \"7101 is 3rd in B2:B6\". INDEX says \"give me the 3rd item of A2:A6\".<\/div>\n<p>MATCH returns a position <em>inside the range you gave<\/em>, not the sheet row number. <code>MATCH(\"Marker\", A2:A6, 0)<\/code> returns 3 even though Marker is on row 4.<\/p>\n\n<h3>4. Two-way lookup<\/h3>\n<p>To find the value at a row label and a column label (region and quarter), use two MATCHes:<\/p>\n<div class=\"fx\">=INDEX(B2:E4, MATCH(\"South\",A2:A4,0), MATCH(\"Q3\",B1:E1,0))<\/div>\n<p>The first MATCH picks the row, the second picks the column, and INDEX returns the cell at their crossing.<\/p>\n\n<h3>5. XLOOKUP: the modern replacement<\/h3>\n<div class=\"fx\">=XLOOKUP(key, lookup_range, return_range, \"if not found\")<\/div>\n<p>It looks in one range and returns from another, left or right, with a built-in not-found message, and defaults to exact match. Use it whenever the question or tool allows.<\/p>\n\n<h3>6. Exact vs approximate match<\/h3>\n<p><b>Exact<\/b> (<code>FALSE<\/code> or <code>0<\/code>): find the key or fail with #N/A. Use for IDs, codes and names.<\/p>\n<p><b>Approximate<\/b> (<code>TRUE<\/code> or <code>1<\/code>): find the <em>largest value that does not exceed<\/em> the key. This is what you want for <em>bands<\/em>: tax slabs, commission tiers, grades. The table must be sorted ascending by its lower limits.<\/p>\n<table>\n<tr><th>Sales from<\/th><th>Rate<\/th><\/tr>\n<tr><td>0<\/td><td>0%<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>50,000<\/td><td>2%<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>1,00,000<\/td><td>3.5%<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>2,00,000<\/td><td>5%<\/td><\/tr>\n<\/table>\n<p>Sales of 1,37,500: the largest limit not above it is 1,00,000, so the rate is 3.5%. <code>=VLOOKUP(137500, A2:B5, 2, TRUE)<\/code>.<\/p>\n<div class=\"trap\"><b>Watch out.<\/b> Approximate match on an unsorted table returns wrong rows without any error. And a band lookup returns the <em>band rate<\/em>, which is not the same as the total tax across slabs. For progressive tax, compute each slab's slice separately (or use SUMPRODUCT).<\/div>\n\n<h3>7. Joins: bringing two tables together<\/h3>\n<p>To attach price to each order line, add a column to the order table that looks up the order's code in the price list. This is a \"left join\": every order row is kept; the price is pulled from the other table.<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>One formula per row, copied down, with the price table locked: <code>=INDEX($B$2:$B$5, MATCH(D2,$A$2:$A$5,0))<\/code>.<\/li>\n<li>If a missing key is expected, use XLOOKUP's not-found argument or <code>IFNA(...)<\/code>. Do not hide all errors by habit.<\/li>\n<li>Duplicate keys: lookups return only the first match. Check with <code>=COUNTIF(A:A, A2)&gt;1<\/code>.<\/li>\n<li>For a total without helper columns: <code>=SUMPRODUCT(SUMIF(codes, order_codes, prices), quantities)<\/code>.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n\n<h3>8. Why lookups fail (diagnose in this order)<\/h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Trailing or leading spaces in the key. Fix with <code>TRIM<\/code>.<\/li>\n<li>Number stored as text on one side, real number on the other.<\/li>\n<li>Table range not locked with <code>$<\/code>, so it slid down as the formula was copied.<\/li>\n<li>Approximate match left on by accident (the last argument was omitted).<\/li>\n<li>Key not in the first column (VLOOKUP).<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<div class=\"try\"><b>Try it.<\/b> Build a five-row price list and an order list. Add a price column using INDEX/MATCH, then break it on purpose by adding a space after one code. Watch #N/A appear, then fix it with TRIM.<\/div>\n"}, {"id": "sm-fin", "sec": "SM", "title": "Financial Functions", "r1": "Financial Functions", "min": 30, "html": "\n<p class=\"lead\">Financial functions are shortcuts for the time value of money. To use them correctly you only need to be sure of three things: what the cash flow signs mean, what one \"period\" is, and which of rate, periods, payment, present value and future value is the unknown.<\/p>\n\n<h3>1. The idea behind all of them<\/h3>\n<p>Money has a time value: ₹100 today can be invested and become more. Five numbers describe any loan or investment with regular payments:<\/p>\n<table>\n<tr><th>Symbol<\/th><th>Meaning<\/th><th>Function argument<\/th><\/tr>\n<tr><td>rate<\/td><td>interest per period<\/td><td><code>rate<\/code><\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>nper<\/td><td>number of periods<\/td><td><code>nper<\/code><\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>pmt<\/td><td>payment each period<\/td><td><code>pmt<\/code><\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>pv<\/td><td>value today<\/td><td><code>pv<\/code><\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>fv<\/td><td>value at the end<\/td><td><code>fv<\/code><\/td><\/tr>\n<\/table>\n<p>Give a function four of these and it solves for the fifth. <code>PMT<\/code> solves for payment, <code>FV<\/code> for future value, <code>PV<\/code> for present value, <code>NPER<\/code> for number of periods, <code>RATE<\/code> for the rate.<\/p>\n<div class=\"why\"><b>Sign convention.<\/b> Think of your bank balance. Money you <em>receive<\/em> is positive; money you <em>pay out<\/em> is negative. A loan you take is +, and the EMIs you pay are −. A SIP you invest is −, and the maturity you receive is +. If your answer has the right size but a minus sign, flip it with a leading minus: <code>=-PMT(...)<\/code>.<\/div>\n\n<h3>2. Match the rate to the period<\/h3>\n<p>The most common error: using an annual rate with monthly payments. If payments are monthly, the rate must be the <em>monthly<\/em> rate (annual ÷ 12) and the periods the <em>number of months<\/em> (years × 12).<\/p>\n<div class=\"fx\">=PMT(9%/12, 5*12, -2500000) &rarr; 51,893 (EMI on ₹25 lakh at 9% for 5 years)<\/div>\n\n<h3>3. The functions<\/h3>\n<table>\n<tr><th>Function<\/th><th>Syntax<\/th><th>Solves for<\/th><\/tr>\n<tr><td>PMT<\/td><td><code>PMT(rate, nper, pv, [fv], [type])<\/code><\/td><td>EMI, or the SIP needed for a goal<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>FV<\/td><td><code>FV(rate, nper, pmt, [pv], [type])<\/code><\/td><td>Corpus after n periods<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>PV<\/td><td><code>PV(rate, nper, pmt, [fv], [type])<\/code><\/td><td>Loan you can afford; value today of a future stream<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>NPER<\/td><td><code>NPER(rate, pmt, pv, [fv])<\/code><\/td><td>Months to clear a loan or reach a goal<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>RATE<\/td><td><code>RATE(nper, pmt, pv, [fv])<\/code><\/td><td>Interest rate per period<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>IPMT / PPMT<\/td><td><code>IPMT(rate, per, nper, pv)<\/code><\/td><td>Interest / principal in one particular instalment<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>NPV<\/td><td><code>NPV(rate, flow1, flow2, ...)<\/code><\/td><td>PV of uneven flows from period 1<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>IRR<\/td><td><code>IRR(flows)<\/code><\/td><td>Rate that makes NPV zero<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>XNPV / XIRR<\/td><td><code>XIRR(flows, dates)<\/code><\/td><td>Same, but on irregular dates<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>EFFECT / NOMINAL<\/td><td><code>EFFECT(nominal, m)<\/code><\/td><td>Converts annual rate with m compoundings to effective rate<\/td><\/tr>\n<\/table>\n\n<h3>4. Worked examples<\/h3>\n<div class=\"eg\"><b>EMI.<\/b> Loan ₹25,00,000 at 9% a year for 5 years, monthly payments.\n<div class=\"fx\">=-PMT(9%/12, 60, 2500000) &rarr; ₹51,893<\/div><\/div>\n<div class=\"eg\"><b>SIP maturity.<\/b> ₹5,000 invested at the end of each month for 10 years at 12% a year (1% a month).\n<div class=\"fx\">=-FV(1%, 120, -5000) &rarr; about ₹11.5 lakh<\/div>\nBecause the payment is negative (money going out), FV comes back positive.<\/div>\n<div class=\"eg\"><b>SIP needed for a goal.<\/b> ₹1 crore in 15 years at 12%.\n<div class=\"fx\">=-PMT(1%, 180, 0, 10000000) &rarr; about ₹20,000 a month<\/div>\npv is 0 (you start with nothing) and fv is the target.<\/div>\n<div class=\"eg\"><b>Months to clear a loan.<\/b> ₹3,00,000 at 18% (1.5% a month) with ₹15,000 a month.\n<div class=\"fx\">=NPER(1.5%, -15000, 300000) &rarr; about 24 months<\/div><\/div>\n<div class=\"eg\"><b>Interest in a given month.<\/b> ₹10 lakh at 10% for 5 years: interest in the first EMI is <code>=-IPMT(10%/12, 1, 60, 1000000)<\/code> = ₹8,333, which is just 10 lakh × 0.8333%. Later instalments carry less interest and more principal.<\/div>\n\n<h3>5. Annuity due vs ordinary annuity<\/h3>\n<p>The optional <code>type<\/code> argument: <code>0<\/code> (or omitted) means payments at the <em>end<\/em> of each period, <code>1<\/code> means at the <em>start<\/em>. A SIP debited on the 1st for the month ahead is closer to type 1. Payments at the start earn one extra period of interest, so type 1 gives a larger FV.<\/p>\n\n<h3>6. NPV, IRR and payback for investments<\/h3>\n<p><b>NPV<\/b> adds up all future cash flows after discounting each back to today at your required return. A positive NPV means the project earns more than that return, so accept it.<\/p>\n<div class=\"fx\">=NPV(rate, C2:C6) + C1 &nbsp; where C1 is today's outflow (negative)<\/div>\n<div class=\"trap\"><b>The NPV trap.<\/b> Excel's and Sheets' <code>NPV<\/code> treats the first number as arriving at the end of period 1. Your period-0 outlay therefore must be added <em>outside<\/em> the function. Putting it inside discounts it by a year and gives the wrong answer.<\/div>\n<p><b>IRR<\/b> is the discount rate at which NPV is exactly zero. If IRR is above your required return, accept. IRR needs at least one negative and one positive flow; otherwise you get #NUM!.<\/p>\n<p><b>Payback period<\/b> has no function. Build a row of cumulative cash flow and find where it first becomes positive. If cumulative cash is −200 at the end of year 3 and year 4 brings in 500, payback is 3 + 200/500 = 3.4 years.<\/p>\n\n<h3>7. Effective vs nominal rates<\/h3>\n<p>A nominal rate of 12% compounded monthly is not 12% a year in effect, because interest earns interest. The effective annual rate is (1 + 0.12/12)^12 − 1 = 12.68%. Use <code>=EFFECT(12%, 12)<\/code>. When comparing two loans or deposits, compare effective rates.<\/p>\n\n<h3>Common traps<\/h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Annual rate with monthly periods.<\/li>\n<li>Sign errors: negative EMI, or FV that comes out as zero because both pmt and pv were negative.<\/li>\n<li>Period-0 cash flow inside NPV.<\/li>\n<li>Wrong <code>type<\/code> for annuity due.<\/li>\n<li>Reading IRR on a project with several sign changes (it may have more than one answer).<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<div class=\"try\"><b>Try it.<\/b> Build a loan schedule: month in column A, opening balance, interest (<code>=B2*rate/12<\/code>), principal (<code>=EMI-interest<\/code>), closing balance. After month 60 the closing balance should be zero. If it is not, your EMI or rate period is wrong.<\/div>\n"}, {"id": "sm-dyn", "sec": "SM", "title": "Dynamic Arrays", "r1": "Dynamic Arrays", "min": 20, "html": "\n<p class=\"lead\">In a classic formula, one cell returns one value. A dynamic-array formula returns a whole list or table from a single cell, and that result \"spills\" into the empty cells next to it. Change the data and the spill grows or shrinks by itself.<\/p>\n\n<h3>1. The idea<\/h3>\n<p>Think of a normal formula as a vending machine that gives you one item. An array formula is a conveyor belt: you ask once and a whole row of items rolls out. The cell holding the formula is the <b>anchor<\/b>; the cells it fills are the <b>spill range<\/b>.<\/p>\n<div class=\"why\"><b>Why it matters.<\/b> Before dynamic arrays you wrote a formula, copied it down 500 rows, and had to remember to extend it when data grew. Now you write it once and the range resizes. This also removes a major source of errors in model design: the \"copied down but missed the last row\" mistake.<\/div>\n<p>Rules for spilling: the cells it needs must be empty, otherwise you get <b>#SPILL!<\/b>. You edit the formula only in the anchor cell. In Excel you can point at the whole spill with <code>A2#<\/code>. In Google Sheets, functions like FILTER, SORT, UNIQUE and SEQUENCE spill natively, and <code>ARRAYFORMULA<\/code> forces ordinary formulas to work on whole ranges.<\/p>\n\n<h3>2. The core functions<\/h3>\n<table>\n<tr><th>Function<\/th><th>What it does<\/th><th>Example<\/th><\/tr>\n<tr><td>FILTER<\/td><td>keeps rows that meet a test<\/td><td><code>=FILTER(A2:C100, C2:C100&gt;50000, \"none\")<\/code><\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>SORT<\/td><td>orders rows by a column<\/td><td><code>=SORT(A2:B100, 2, FALSE)<\/code> (column 2, largest first)<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>SORTN (Sheets)<\/td><td>sorts then keeps the top n<\/td><td><code>=SORTN(A2:B100, 5, 0, 2, FALSE)<\/code><\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>UNIQUE<\/td><td>removes duplicates<\/td><td><code>=UNIQUE(B2:B100)<\/code><\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>SEQUENCE<\/td><td>generates numbers<\/td><td><code>=SEQUENCE(12)<\/code> gives 1 to 12; <code>=SEQUENCE(5,1,100,10)<\/code> gives 100,110,...<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>TRANSPOSE<\/td><td>swaps rows and columns<\/td><td><code>=TRANSPOSE(A1:A5)<\/code><\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>ARRAYFORMULA (Sheets)<\/td><td>applies a formula to a whole range<\/td><td><code>=ARRAYFORMULA(B2:B*C2:C)<\/code><\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>LET (Excel)<\/td><td>names a sub-result inside a formula<\/td><td><code>=LET(r, B1/12, B2*r)<\/code><\/td><\/tr>\n<\/table>\n\n<h3>3. FILTER in depth<\/h3>\n<p>FILTER takes two main inputs: <b>what to return<\/b> and <b>a test of the same height<\/b>. The test produces TRUE/FALSE for each row; only TRUE rows survive.<\/p>\n<div class=\"eg\">Region in column A, Rep in column B, Revenue in column C.\n<div class=\"fx\">=FILTER(A2:C100, A2:A100=\"North\")<\/div>\nreturns all columns for North rows. To combine tests, multiply for AND, add for OR:\n<div class=\"fx\">AND: =FILTER(A2:C100, (A2:A100=\"North\")*(C2:C100&gt;1000))<br>OR: =FILTER(A2:C100, (A2:A100=\"North\")+(A2:A100=\"East\"))<\/div>\nMultiplication works because TRUE × TRUE = 1 and anything × FALSE = 0.<\/div>\n<p>If nothing passes the test FILTER gives #N/A. Always add the third argument: <code>=FILTER(range, test, \"none\")<\/code>.<\/p>\n\n<h3>4. Building up answers by nesting<\/h3>\n<p>The result of one array function can feed another. Read from the inside out.<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Number of distinct customers:<\/b> <code>=COUNTA(UNIQUE(B2:B100))<\/code>.<\/li>\n<li><b>Total revenue for North without SUMIF:<\/b> <code>=SUM(FILTER(C2:C100, A2:A100=\"North\"))<\/code>.<\/li>\n<li><b>Highest North revenue:<\/b> <code>=MAX(FILTER(C2:C100, A2:A100=\"North\"))<\/code>.<\/li>\n<li><b>Top 5 rows by revenue:<\/b> <code>=SORTN(A2:C100, 5, 0, 3, FALSE)<\/code> in Sheets, or <code>=TAKE(SORT(A2:C100,3,-1),5)<\/code> in Excel 365.<\/li>\n<li><b>Items on list 1 missing from list 2:<\/b> <code>=FILTER(A2:A50, ISNA(MATCH(A2:A50, D2:D40, 0)))<\/code>.<\/li>\n<li><b>A 60-month schedule's month column:<\/b> <code>=SEQUENCE(60)<\/code>.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n\n<h3>5. Array arithmetic and SUMPRODUCT<\/h3>\n<p>Operations on ranges work element by element. <code>B2:B5*C2:C5<\/code> multiplies row by row. <code>SUMPRODUCT<\/code> does the multiply-and-add in one step and works in every version, even where spilling does not:<\/p>\n<div class=\"fx\">=SUMPRODUCT((A2:A100=\"North\")*(C2:C100&gt;1000)*D2:D100)<\/div>\n<p>This reads: for each row, 1 if North and over 1000 else 0, times the amount, then add them all up.<\/p>\n\n<h3>6. Exam-style traps<\/h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Pointing at a single spill cell with a fixed address (like <code>E5<\/code>) so the model breaks when the spill grows. Reference the whole spill.<\/li>\n<li>Forgetting that the column index in SORT counts <em>within the range given<\/em>, not the sheet.<\/li>\n<li>Having non-empty cells in the spill zone (#SPILL!).<\/li>\n<li>Using FILTER without a not-found value.<\/li>\n<li>In Sheets, writing <code>=B2:B*C2:C<\/code> without ARRAYFORMULA and getting only one row.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<div class=\"try\"><b>Try it.<\/b> Paste 20 sales lines (region, rep, revenue). In one cell show the unique regions; beside it, in one cell per region, the total using SUMIF; then rebuild the same total with <code>SUM(FILTER(...))<\/code>. The answers must match.<\/div>\n"}, {"id": "sm-model", "sec": "SM", "title": "Model Design", "r1": "Model Design", "min": 25, "html": "\n<p class=\"lead\">A model is not judged on whether it gives today's answer. It is judged on whether a stranger can change an assumption and trust what comes out. Good design is a small set of habits that make errors visible and changes cheap.<\/p>\n\n<h3>1. Why design matters<\/h3>\n<p>Studies of real business spreadsheets repeatedly find errors in a large majority of them. The errors are rarely in the maths. They come from hard-coded numbers, inconsistent formulas, and inputs scattered across sheets. A well-designed model makes each of those hard to commit.<\/p>\n\n<h3>2. Separate inputs, calculations and outputs<\/h3>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Inputs<\/b>: every assumption (price, growth, tax rate, loan amount) lives in one clearly labelled area, one value per cell, with a unit.<\/li>\n<li><b>Calculations<\/b>: formulas that read inputs and produce intermediate results. No typed numbers.<\/li>\n<li><b>Outputs<\/b>: summary, charts, and decisions that read from calculations.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>Convention: blue font for inputs, black for formulas, green for links to other sheets. A reader can then see at a glance what they are allowed to change.<\/p>\n<div class=\"why\"><b>The test.<\/b> Change one input. If the answer does not move, there is a hard-code somewhere. If the answer moves in the wrong direction, there is a logic error. This is the fastest audit you can do.<\/div>\n\n<h3>3. No hard-codes<\/h3>\n<p>A hard-code is a number typed inside a formula: <code>=B14*0.25<\/code>. It hides the assumption, and it is the usual reason a model is wrong after one input changes. Fix: put 25% in an input cell labelled \"Tax rate\" and write <code>=B14*$C$3<\/code>, or name the cell <code>TaxRate<\/code> and write <code>=B14*TaxRate<\/code>.<\/p>\n\n<h3>4. Consistent formulas<\/h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Time runs left to right, one period per column, and means the same on every sheet.<\/li>\n<li>One formula per row, copied across all periods. If one cell differs, someone has overridden it; the row is no longer trustworthy.<\/li>\n<li>If a period really needs different logic (a one-off cost), model it in a separate row or input rather than overwriting a cell.<\/li>\n<li>No blank rows or merged cells inside data ranges; they break sorting, filtering and array formulas.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n\n<h3>5. Checks and error handling<\/h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Build <b>check cells<\/b> that must equal zero: assets − (liabilities + equity); sum of parts − total; closing cash on the cash flow − cash on the balance sheet. Show one master check at the top.<\/li>\n<li>Use <b>data validation<\/b> to stop impossible inputs: a dropdown list for scenarios, percentages between 0 and 100%.<\/li>\n<li>Use <code>IFERROR<\/code> only where an error is expected and the replacement value is meaningful. Wrapping everything in IFERROR hides real data problems.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n\n<h3>6. Scenarios and sensitivity<\/h3>\n<p><b>Scenarios<\/b>: a selector cell (1 = base, 2 = upside, 3 = downside) drives <code>=CHOOSE(selector, base, upside, downside)<\/code> or <code>=INDEX(scenario_row, selector)<\/code> for each key input. One switch flips the whole model.<\/p>\n<p><b>Sensitivity<\/b>: shows how an output reacts as one or two inputs change. A one-way test varies one input. A two-way table puts one input down the side and another across the top with the output in the corner, for example NPV against discount rate and growth. Excel has Data Tables; in Sheets build the grid with formulas.<\/p>\n<p><b>Goal Seek<\/b> works backwards: \"what price makes profit exactly zero?\" It adjusts one input until an output hits a target value.<\/p>\n\n<h3>7. Documentation<\/h3>\n<ul>\n<li>A cover or notes area stating purpose, units (₹ lakh, ₹ crore, monthly or annual), version and key assumptions.<\/li>\n<li>Named ranges for key inputs so formulas read like sentences.<\/li>\n<li>Units in headers; consistent number formats; frozen header rows; comments where logic is not obvious.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n\n<h3>8. A model-review checklist<\/h3>\n<table>\n<tr><th>Question<\/th><th>Good sign<\/th><th>Red flag<\/th><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Where are assumptions?<\/td><td>One labelled block<\/td><td>Scattered through formulas<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Typed numbers in formulas?<\/td><td>None<\/td><td><code>*0.25<\/code>, <code>/12<\/code> for rates<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Is each row one formula?<\/td><td>Yes, copied across<\/td><td>One cell overwritten<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Do totals tie?<\/td><td>Check cell = 0<\/td><td>No checks at all<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Is the sheet auditable?<\/td><td>Clear flow top to bottom<\/td><td>Circular references, merged cells<\/td><\/tr>\n<\/table>\n\n<div class=\"eg\"><b>Spot the flaws.<\/b> A model types <code>*0.25<\/code> in twenty formulas for tax, has year-4 growth overwritten with 12%, and uses <code>=SUM(C5:C14)<\/code> for a total that should include row 15. Fixes: move 25% to an input cell and lock it; restore the growth formula and put any year-4 override in a scenario input; extend the SUM to row 15 (or use a table so the range grows).<\/div>\n<div class=\"try\"><b>Try it.<\/b> Take any calculation you have built (the EMI schedule from the Financial Functions topic). Move every number into an Inputs block, add a check that the final balance is zero, and add a scenario selector for three interest rates.<\/div>\n"}, {"id": "bf-fs", "sec": "BF", "title": "Financial Statements", "r1": "Financial Statements", "min": 40, "html": "\n<p class=\"lead\">Financial statements are the scoreboard of a business. There are three, and each answers one question: Did we make a profit? (income statement) What do we own and owe? (balance sheet) Where did the cash go? (cash flow statement). This topic builds all three from scratch using one small company, so you can see how a single event flows through every statement.<\/p>\n\n<h3>1. The accounting equation: where everything starts<\/h3>\n<div class=\"fx\">Assets = Liabilities + Equity<\/div>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Assets<\/b>: things the business owns that will bring future benefit (cash, stock, equipment, money customers owe).<\/li>\n<li><b>Liabilities<\/b>: what it owes to outsiders (suppliers, banks).<\/li>\n<li><b>Equity<\/b>: what is left for the owners: money they put in plus profits kept in the business.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<div class=\"why\"><b>Why it always balances.<\/b> Every asset was paid for either with someone else's money (liability) or the owners' money (equity). Rearranged: Equity = Assets − Liabilities, the owners' claim on what remains. Every transaction changes at least two items so that the equation stays true. This is double-entry bookkeeping. If you ever see a balance sheet that does not balance, an entry is missing.<\/div>\n<p>Quick transactions to feel it:<\/p>\n<table>\n<tr><th>Event<\/th><th>Assets<\/th><th>Liabilities<\/th><th>Equity<\/th><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Owner puts in ₹100 cash<\/td><td>Cash +100<\/td><td><\/td><td>Capital +100<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Bank lends ₹80<\/td><td>Cash +80<\/td><td>Loan +80<\/td><td><\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Buy equipment for ₹120 cash<\/td><td>Equipment +120, Cash −120<\/td><td><\/td><td><\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Sell goods on credit for ₹60<\/td><td>Receivables +60<\/td><td><\/td><td>Profit +60 (revenue)<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Pay supplier owed ₹50<\/td><td>Cash −50<\/td><td>Payables −50<\/td><td><\/td><\/tr>\n<\/table>\n\n<h3>2. The income statement (a period)<\/h3>\n<p>It measures performance <em>over a period<\/em> (a quarter, a year): revenue earned, less costs incurred to earn it. It is built on <b>accrual accounting<\/b>: revenue is recorded when earned and costs when incurred, <em>not<\/em> when cash moves. That is why profit and cash differ.<\/p>\n<div class=\"fx\">Revenue<br>&minus; Cost of goods sold (COGS) &rarr; <b>Gross profit<\/b><br>&minus; Operating expenses (salaries, rent, marketing) and depreciation &rarr; <b>EBIT<\/b> (operating profit)<br>&minus; Interest &rarr; <b>PBT<\/b> (profit before tax)<br>&minus; Tax &rarr; <b>PAT<\/b> (net profit)<\/div>\n<ul>\n<li><b>COGS<\/b> is the cost of the goods <em>actually sold<\/em>, not bought. Opening stock + purchases − closing stock.<\/li>\n<li><b>Depreciation<\/b> spreads the cost of equipment over its useful life. A ₹120 lakh machine lasting 10 years costs ₹12 lakh a year in profit terms. It is a real expense but <em>no cash leaves<\/em> in that year.<\/li>\n<li><b>EBITDA<\/b> = EBIT + depreciation + amortisation. A rough measure of operating cash earning power before capital costs.<\/li>\n<li><b>EPS<\/b> (earnings per share) = PAT ÷ shares outstanding.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n\n<h3>3. The balance sheet (a date)<\/h3>\n<p>A snapshot on one date. Assets on one side; liabilities plus equity on the other; the two sides are equal.<\/p>\n<table>\n<tr><th>Assets<\/th><th>Liabilities and equity<\/th><\/tr>\n<tr><td><b>Current assets<\/b> (expected to turn to cash in 12 months): cash, receivables, inventory<\/td><td><b>Current liabilities<\/b> (due within 12 months): payables, short-term loans, accrued expenses<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td><b>Non-current assets<\/b>: equipment, property (net of depreciation), intangibles, long-term investments<\/td><td><b>Non-current liabilities<\/b>: long-term loans, lease liabilities, deferred tax<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td><\/td><td><b>Equity<\/b>: share capital + reserves and retained earnings<\/td><\/tr>\n<\/table>\n<p><b>Working capital<\/b> = current assets − current liabilities. It is the cushion of short-term resources over short-term bills. <b>Net debt<\/b> = borrowings − cash.<\/p>\n<p><b>Retained earnings<\/b> = opening retained earnings + PAT − dividends. Profit that is not paid out is added to the owners' claim.<\/p>\n\n<h3>4. The cash flow statement (a period)<\/h3>\n<p>Profit is an opinion built on accounting rules; cash is a fact. The cash flow statement reconciles the two and shows <em>where<\/em> cash came from and went.<\/p>\n<table>\n<tr><th>Section<\/th><th>What it contains<\/th><\/tr>\n<tr><td><b>Operating<\/b><\/td><td>Cash from running the business. Start from PAT, add back non-cash items (depreciation), then adjust for working-capital changes.<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td><b>Investing<\/b><\/td><td>Buying or selling long-term assets: capex (outflow), sale of equipment (inflow), buying investments.<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td><b>Financing<\/b><\/td><td>Raising or repaying money: equity raised, loans taken or repaid, dividends paid.<\/td><\/tr>\n<\/table>\n<p><b>The working-capital rules (memorise as a direction table):<\/b><\/p>\n<table>\n<tr><th>Item rises<\/th><th>Effect on cash<\/th><th>Reason<\/th><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Receivables<\/td><td>cash falls<\/td><td>you sold but have not been paid<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Inventory<\/td><td>cash falls<\/td><td>cash is tied up in stock<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Payables<\/td><td>cash rises<\/td><td>you have used goods without yet paying<\/td><\/tr>\n<\/table>\n<p><b>Free cash flow<\/b> = operating cash flow − capex. It is what is truly left for lenders and owners.<\/p>\n\n<h3>5. One company, three statements<\/h3>\n<div class=\"eg\"><b>Kaveri Bakes, Year 1 (₹ lakh).<\/b>\n<ol>\n<li>Owners put in ₹100. A bank lends ₹80 at 10%. Cash = 180.<\/li>\n<li>Buys equipment ₹120 (life 10 years, so depreciation ₹12 a year). Cash = 60.<\/li>\n<li>Sales ₹400: ₹340 collected, ₹60 still owed by customers.<\/li>\n<li>Buys ingredients ₹250: pays ₹200, owes ₹50. Closing stock ₹30, so COGS = 250 − 30 = 220.<\/li>\n<li>Pays operating expenses ₹80, interest ₹8 (10% × 80), tax ₹20.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<b>Income statement<\/b>\n<table>\n<tr><td>Revenue<\/td><td>400<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>COGS<\/td><td>(220)<\/td><\/tr><tr><td><b>Gross profit<\/b><\/td><td><b>180<\/b><\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Operating expenses<\/td><td>(80)<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Depreciation<\/td><td>(12)<\/td><\/tr><tr><td><b>EBIT<\/b><\/td><td><b>88<\/b><\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Interest<\/td><td>(8)<\/td><\/tr><tr><td><b>PBT<\/b><\/td><td><b>80<\/b><\/td><\/tr><tr><td>Tax at 25%<\/td><td>(20)<\/td><\/tr><tr><td><b>PAT<\/b><\/td><td><b>60<\/b><\/td><\/tr>\n<\/table>\n<b>Cash flow statement<\/b>\n<table>\n<tr><td>PAT<\/td><td>60<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>+ Depreciation (non-cash)<\/td><td>12<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>&minus; Increase in receivables<\/td><td>(60)<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>&minus; Increase in inventory<\/td><td>(30)<\/td><\/tr><tr><td>+ Increase in payables<\/td><td>50<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td><b>Operating cash flow<\/b><\/td><td><b>32<\/b><\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Capex (equipment)<\/td><td>(120)<\/td><\/tr><tr><td><b>Investing<\/b><\/td><td><b>(120)<\/b><\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Equity 100 + Loan 80<\/td><td>180<\/td><\/tr><tr><td><b>Net change in cash<\/b><\/td><td><b>92<\/b><\/td><\/tr>\n<\/table>\n<b>Balance sheet at year end<\/b>\n<table>\n<tr><th>Assets<\/th><th><\/th><th>Liabilities and equity<\/th><th><\/th><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Cash<\/td><td>92<\/td><td>Payables<\/td><td>50<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Receivables<\/td><td>60<\/td><td>Loan<\/td><td>80<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Inventory<\/td><td>30<\/td><td>Share capital<\/td><td>100<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Equipment (120 &minus; 12)<\/td><td>108<\/td><td>Retained earnings<\/td><td>60<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td><b>Total<\/b><\/td><td><b>290<\/b><\/td><td><b>Total<\/b><\/td><td><b>290<\/b><\/td><\/tr>\n<\/table>\nNotice: profit is ₹60 lakh but operating cash flow is only ₹32 lakh. The gap is the money stuck in customers' hands (60) and stock (30), partly funded by suppliers (50), plus depreciation added back (12). Profitable businesses run out of cash when they grow receivables and stock faster than they collect.<\/div>\n\n<h3>6. How the statements link<\/h3>\n<ul>\n<li>PAT on the income statement flows into retained earnings on the balance sheet, and is the first line of the cash flow statement.<\/li>\n<li>Closing cash on the cash flow statement equals cash on the balance sheet.<\/li>\n<li>Depreciation lowers profit and lowers the equipment on the balance sheet, but never touches cash directly.<\/li>\n<li>Capex does not hit the income statement at once; it becomes an asset and is expensed gradually as depreciation.<\/li>\n<li>Borrowing adds cash and a liability; principal repayment reduces both; interest hits profit.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n\n<h3>7. Accounting judgement<\/h3>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Revenue recognition<\/b>: record when the performance obligation is met, not when cash arrives. Advance received is a liability (deferred revenue) until delivered.<\/li>\n<li><b>Capitalise vs expense<\/b>: a cost that gives benefit over several years (machine) is an asset; day-to-day running costs are expensed. Capitalising too much flatters profit now.<\/li>\n<li><b>Contingent liabilities<\/b> (pending lawsuits): recognised as a liability if a loss is probable and measurable; otherwise disclosed in notes.<\/li>\n<li><b>Depreciation methods<\/b>: straight line (equal each year) vs reducing balance (higher early). Total depreciation over life is the same; timing differs.<\/li>\n<li><b>Inventory valuation<\/b> (FIFO, weighted average) changes COGS and profit when prices move.<\/li>\n<li><b>Provisions and impairment<\/b>: expected losses reduce profit before cash is lost.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n\n<h3>Common traps<\/h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Treating depreciation as a cash cost, or forgetting to add it back.<\/li>\n<li>Putting dividends in operating cash flow (they are financing).<\/li>\n<li>Confusing COGS with purchases (use opening + purchases − closing).<\/li>\n<li>Calling a loan repayment an expense (principal is not an expense; interest is).<\/li>\n<li>Reading higher receivables as \"more cash\".<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n"}, {"id": "bf-ratio", "sec": "BF", "title": "Ratios and performance", "r1": "Ratios and performance", "min": 35, "html": "\n<p class=\"lead\">A raw number such as \"profit ₹60 lakh\" says little. Is that good for a business with ₹400 lakh of sales? For ₹4,000 lakh? A ratio puts one figure against another so businesses of different sizes, and the same business in different years, can be compared. We use Kaveri Bakes from the previous topic: revenue 400, COGS 220, EBIT 88, interest 8, PAT 60, cash 92, receivables 60, inventory 30, equipment 108, total assets 290, payables 50, loan 80, equity 160.<\/p>\n\n<h3>1. Five questions, five families<\/h3>\n<ol>\n<li><b>Can it pay its bills?<\/b> Liquidity ratios.<\/li>\n<li><b>Does it make money on each sale?<\/b> Margins.<\/li>\n<li><b>How well does it use what it has?<\/b> Efficiency (turnover and days).<\/li>\n<li><b>How much does it earn for its owners?<\/b> Returns.<\/li>\n<li><b>How risky is the way it is funded?<\/b> Leverage and coverage.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n\n<h3>2. Liquidity: short-term survival<\/h3>\n<table>\n<tr><th>Ratio<\/th><th>Formula<\/th><th>Kaveri<\/th><th>Meaning<\/th><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Current ratio<\/td><td>Current assets ÷ current liabilities<\/td><td>182 ÷ 50 = 3.64<\/td><td>Rupees of short-term assets per rupee of short-term bills<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Quick ratio<\/td><td>(Current assets − inventory) ÷ current liabilities<\/td><td>152 ÷ 50 = 3.04<\/td><td>Same, ignoring stock that may be slow to sell<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Cash ratio<\/td><td>Cash ÷ current liabilities<\/td><td>92 ÷ 50 = 1.84<\/td><td>Strictest test<\/td><\/tr>\n<\/table>\n<p>A ratio below 1 means short-term bills exceed short-term assets, a warning sign for most firms. But very high is not automatically good: it can mean idle cash or bloated stock.<\/p>\n\n<h3>3. Margins: profit per rupee of sales<\/h3>\n<table>\n<tr><th>Ratio<\/th><th>Formula<\/th><th>Kaveri<\/th><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Gross margin<\/td><td>Gross profit ÷ revenue<\/td><td>180 ÷ 400 = 45%<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>EBITDA margin<\/td><td>EBITDA ÷ revenue<\/td><td>100 ÷ 400 = 25%<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Operating (EBIT) margin<\/td><td>EBIT ÷ revenue<\/td><td>88 ÷ 400 = 22%<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Net margin<\/td><td>PAT ÷ revenue<\/td><td>60 ÷ 400 = 15%<\/td><\/tr>\n<\/table>\n<p>Gross margin shows pricing power and production cost. EBIT margin shows how well the whole operation turns sales into profit before financing. Net margin is what survives interest and tax. A falling gross margin points to costs rising faster than prices; a falling EBIT margin with stable gross margin points to overheads.<\/p>\n\n<h3>4. Efficiency: how hard assets work<\/h3>\n<table>\n<tr><th>Ratio<\/th><th>Formula<\/th><th>Kaveri<\/th><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Receivable days<\/td><td>Receivables ÷ revenue × 365<\/td><td>60 ÷ 400 × 365 = 54.8 days<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Inventory days<\/td><td>Inventory ÷ COGS × 365<\/td><td>30 ÷ 220 × 365 = 49.8 days<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Payable days<\/td><td>Payables ÷ COGS × 365<\/td><td>50 ÷ 220 × 365 = 83.0 days<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Inventory turnover<\/td><td>COGS ÷ inventory<\/td><td>220 ÷ 30 = 7.3 times<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Asset turnover<\/td><td>Revenue ÷ total assets<\/td><td>400 ÷ 290 = 1.38<\/td><\/tr>\n<\/table>\n<div class=\"why\"><b>Cash conversion cycle = receivable days + inventory days − payable days.<\/b> For Kaveri: 54.8 + 49.8 − 83.0 = 21.6 days. This is how long cash is tied up between paying for ingredients and collecting from customers. A shorter or negative cycle means the business is financed by suppliers; supermarkets often run negative cycles. When the question says \"average\", use (opening + closing) ÷ 2.<\/div>\n<p>Use COGS (not revenue) for inventory and payable days because stock and payables are valued at cost.<\/p>\n\n<h3>5. Returns: payoff for capital providers<\/h3>\n<table>\n<tr><th>Ratio<\/th><th>Formula<\/th><th>Kaveri<\/th><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Return on equity (ROE)<\/td><td>PAT ÷ equity<\/td><td>60 ÷ 160 = 37.5%<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Return on assets (ROA)<\/td><td>PAT ÷ total assets<\/td><td>60 ÷ 290 = 20.7%<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Return on capital employed (ROCE)<\/td><td>EBIT ÷ (total assets − current liabilities)<\/td><td>88 ÷ 240 = 36.7%<\/td><\/tr>\n<\/table>\n<p>ROCE uses EBIT because it measures the return to <em>all<\/em> long-term capital before the split between lenders and owners.<\/p>\n\n<h3>6. DuPont: why is ROE high?<\/h3>\n<div class=\"fx\">ROE = Net margin &times; Asset turnover &times; Equity multiplier<br>= (PAT ÷ Revenue) &times; (Revenue ÷ Assets) &times; (Assets ÷ Equity)<\/div>\n<p>Kaveri: 15% × 1.379 × 1.8125 = 37.5%. The three levers are profitability, efficiency and leverage. Two firms with a 20% ROE can be completely different: a luxury brand (high margin, low turnover), a supermarket (low margin, high turnover) or a highly borrowed firm (low margin and turnover boosted by leverage). Leverage raises ROE in good times and magnifies losses in bad times.<\/p>\n\n<h3>7. Leverage and coverage: long-term risk<\/h3>\n<table>\n<tr><th>Ratio<\/th><th>Formula<\/th><th>Kaveri<\/th><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Debt-to-equity<\/td><td>Borrowings ÷ equity<\/td><td>80 ÷ 160 = 0.5<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Equity multiplier<\/td><td>Total assets ÷ equity<\/td><td>1.81<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Interest coverage<\/td><td>EBIT ÷ interest<\/td><td>88 ÷ 8 = 11 times<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Net debt ÷ EBITDA<\/td><td>(Debt − cash) ÷ EBITDA<\/td><td>(80 − 92) ÷ 100 = −0.12 (net cash)<\/td><\/tr>\n<\/table>\n<p>Interest coverage below about 2 is risky: a modest fall in profit leaves no room to pay lenders.<\/p>\n\n<h3>8. How to read ratios like an analyst<\/h3>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Compare<\/b> with the firm's own history and with similar firms; the \"right\" number depends on the industry.<\/li>\n<li><b>Look at the trend<\/b>, not one year: margins falling while revenue rises means costs are growing faster than sales.<\/li>\n<li><b>Link ratios to causes<\/b>: receivable days rising may mean weak collection or loose credit to push sales; current ratio fine but quick ratio poor means liquidity is trapped in stock.<\/li>\n<li><b>Mind the base<\/b>: a profit of ₹10 on a base of ₹5 is a 200% rise; ratios on tiny bases mislead.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<div class=\"trap\"><b>Common traps.<\/b> Using closing balances when the stem says \"average\". Using revenue instead of COGS for inventory days. Using PAT where the formula wants EBIT (ROCE, coverage). Mixing 360 and 365 days: use whatever the stem says. Giving a percentage answer as a fraction or the reverse.<\/div>\n<div class=\"eg\"><b>Same ROE, different business.<\/b> Firm A: revenue 400, PAT 40, assets 200, equity 80: 10% × 2.0 × 2.5 = 50%. Firm B: 25% margin, 0.4 turnover, multiplier 5: 50% too. B depends on borrowing for its ROE, so a small drop in profit hurts it far more.<\/div>\n"}, {"id": "bf-unit", "sec": "BF", "title": "Unit economics and business models", "r1": "Unit economics and business models", "min": 40, "html": "\n<p class=\"lead\">Before asking whether a company is profitable, ask whether <em>one unit<\/em> of its business makes money. A unit can be one product sold, one delivery, one subscriber or one customer. If a single unit loses money, growth makes losses bigger. Unit economics is the discipline of finding that out.<\/p>\n\n<h3>1. Costs: fixed, variable, and why it matters<\/h3>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Variable costs<\/b> change with volume: ingredients, packaging, delivery fees, sales commission. Per unit they stay roughly the same.<\/li>\n<li><b>Fixed costs<\/b> do not change with volume in the short run: rent, salaries, software subscriptions. Per unit they fall as volume rises.<\/li>\n<li><b>Semi-variable<\/b> costs have both parts (a phone plan with a fixed fee plus per-minute charges).<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<div class=\"why\"><b>The key insight.<\/b> Once variable costs are paid, each extra unit contributes what is left of its price to covering fixed costs. This is why a business with large fixed costs can lose money at low volume and become very profitable at high volume.<\/div>\n\n<h3>2. Contribution and break-even (CVP analysis)<\/h3>\n<div class=\"fx\">Contribution per unit = Price &minus; Variable cost per unit<br>Contribution margin ratio (CM%) = Contribution &divide; Price<br>Break-even units = Fixed costs &divide; Contribution per unit<br>Break-even revenue = Fixed costs &divide; CM%<br>Units for a target profit = (Fixed costs + Target profit) &divide; Contribution per unit<br>Margin of safety = (Actual sales &minus; Break-even sales) &divide; Actual sales<\/div>\n<div class=\"eg\"><b>Tiffin service.<\/b> Price ₹150, variable cost ₹90, fixed costs ₹60,000 a month.\n<ul>\n<li>Contribution = 150 − 90 = ₹60 (CM% = 40%).<\/li>\n<li>Break-even = 60,000 ÷ 60 = 1,000 meals, or ₹1,50,000 of revenue (60,000 ÷ 0.40).<\/li>\n<li>At 1,500 meals: profit = 1,500 × 60 − 60,000 = ₹30,000. Margin of safety = (1,500 − 1,000) ÷ 1,500 = 33%.<\/li>\n<li>For a ₹45,000 profit: (60,000 + 45,000) ÷ 60 = 1,750 meals.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\nRound break-even units <em>up<\/em> to a whole number. Break-even is the point at which profit is exactly zero.<\/div>\n\n<h3>3. Operating leverage<\/h3>\n<div class=\"fx\">Degree of operating leverage (DOL) = Contribution &divide; EBIT<\/div>\n<p>With 1,500 meals: contribution = 90,000, EBIT = 30,000, DOL = 3. A 10% rise in sales gives a 30% rise in profit, and a 10% fall gives a 30% fall. High fixed costs raise operating leverage: more reward in good times, more risk in bad.<\/p>\n\n<h3>4. More than one product<\/h3>\n<p>Use a weighted-average contribution. If you sell products in a fixed mix, treat one \"bundle\" as the unit. Example: Product A (contribution ₹20) and B (contribution ₹50) sold 3 : 1. One bundle contributes 3×20 + 50 = ₹110. Fixed costs ₹55,000 need 500 bundles = 1,500 A and 500 B.<\/p>\n\n<h3>5. Pricing: margin vs markup<\/h3>\n<p>These two are mixed up constantly. <b>Markup<\/b> is profit as a share of <em>cost<\/em>; <b>margin<\/b> is profit as a share of <em>price<\/em>.<\/p>\n<div class=\"fx\">Price = Cost &times; (1 + markup) &nbsp;|&nbsp; Margin = Markup &divide; (1 + markup)<\/div>\n<p>A 25% markup on cost gives a margin of 0.25 ÷ 1.25 = 20%. To get a 20% margin you need a 25% markup. Price discounts hit contribution rupee for rupee: a ₹10 discount on a ₹60 contribution cuts contribution by 17% and raises the break-even volume by 20%.<\/p>\n\n<h3>6. Decisions with unit economics<\/h3>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Special order<\/b>: accept if the price exceeds the extra (variable) cost and spare capacity exists. Fixed costs already being paid are irrelevant.<\/li>\n<li><b>Make or buy<\/b>: compare the avoidable cost of making with the price of buying. Unavoidable fixed costs do not count.<\/li>\n<li><b>Drop a product<\/b>: drop only if its contribution is less than the fixed costs you can actually avoid. A product with negative profit may still contribute to overheads.<\/li>\n<li><b>Limiting factor<\/b>: when one resource is scarce, rank products by contribution <em>per unit of that resource<\/em> (per machine hour), not per unit of product.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<div class=\"why\"><b>Relevant costs.<\/b> Only costs that change because of the decision matter. Sunk costs (already spent) and costs that stay the same either way are ignored.<\/div>\n\n<h3>7. Customer economics (subscription and digital businesses)<\/h3>\n<table>\n<tr><th>Metric<\/th><th>Formula<\/th><th>Meaning<\/th><\/tr>\n<tr><td>ARPU<\/td><td>Revenue ÷ active users<\/td><td>Average revenue per user per period<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Churn rate<\/td><td>Customers lost ÷ customers at start<\/td><td>How fast customers leave<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Customer lifetime<\/td><td>1 ÷ churn rate<\/td><td>5% monthly churn means 20 months on average<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>LTV<\/td><td>ARPU × gross margin ÷ churn<\/td><td>Lifetime profit from a customer<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>CAC<\/td><td>Sales and marketing spend ÷ new customers<\/td><td>Cost to win a customer<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>LTV : CAC<\/td><td>LTV ÷ CAC<\/td><td>About 3 or more is healthy<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>CAC payback<\/td><td>CAC ÷ (monthly ARPU × gross margin)<\/td><td>Months to earn back acquisition cost<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Net revenue retention<\/td><td>(Start + expansion − churn − contraction) ÷ start<\/td><td>Above 100% means existing customers grow<\/td><\/tr>\n<\/table>\n<div class=\"eg\"><b>Subscription app.<\/b> Monthly ARPU ₹400, gross margin 70%, monthly churn 5%, CAC ₹1,400.\n<ul><li>Gross profit per customer per month = 400 × 0.70 = ₹280.<\/li>\n<li>LTV = 280 ÷ 0.05 = ₹5,600.<\/li>\n<li>LTV : CAC = 5,600 ÷ 1,400 = 4.0 (healthy).<\/li>\n<li>Payback = 1,400 ÷ 280 = 5 months.<\/li><\/ul><\/div>\n\n<h3>8. Marketplaces and delivery businesses<\/h3>\n<ul>\n<li><b>GMV<\/b> (gross merchandise value) is the value of goods sold through the platform, not the platform's revenue.<\/li>\n<li><b>Take rate<\/b> = platform revenue ÷ GMV. A 20% take rate on ₹1,000 order is ₹200 revenue.<\/li>\n<li><b>Contribution per order<\/b> = commission + delivery fee + ad revenue − delivery cost − discounts − payment fees. Positive contribution per order is what lets scale eventually cover fixed costs.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<div class=\"eg\">Order value ₹500. Commission 20% (₹100), customer delivery fee ₹30, rider cost ₹55, discount funded by platform ₹25, payment fee 1.5% of ₹500 (₹7.50). Contribution = 100 + 30 − 55 − 25 − 7.5 = ₹42.50 per order. At ₹20 lakh of fixed monthly cost, break-even is about 47,059 orders a month.<\/div>\n\n<h3>9. Business model types<\/h3>\n<table>\n<tr><th>Model<\/th><th>How it makes money<\/th><th>Key number<\/th><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Subscription<\/td><td>Recurring fee<\/td><td>Churn, LTV : CAC<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Marketplace<\/td><td>Commission on transactions<\/td><td>Take rate, orders per user<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Freemium<\/td><td>Small share of free users pay<\/td><td>Conversion to paid<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Razor and blade<\/td><td>Cheap device, profitable consumables<\/td><td>Attach rate, repeat purchase<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Franchise / asset-light<\/td><td>Royalty or fee; partner funds assets<\/td><td>Royalty %, outlet count<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Advertising<\/td><td>Sell users' attention<\/td><td>Engagement, revenue per user<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Manufacturing / retail<\/td><td>Margin on goods<\/td><td>Gross margin, inventory turnover<\/td><\/tr>\n<\/table>\n\n<h3>Common traps<\/h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Using revenue instead of gross profit in LTV.<\/li>\n<li>Mixing monthly churn with annual ARPU.<\/li>\n<li>Rounding break-even down; round up.<\/li>\n<li>Including fixed costs in per-unit cost when asked for contribution.<\/li>\n<li>Confusing markup with margin; confusing GMV with revenue.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n"}, {"id": "bf-bank", "sec": "BF", "title": "Banks and financial institutions", "r1": "Banks and financial institutions", "min": 35, "html": "\n<p class=\"lead\">A bank is a business that borrows from people with spare money and lends to people who need it, earning the difference. Everything else in this topic (ratios, regulation, bad loans) follows from that one fact and from the fact that a bank's failure harms the whole economy.<\/p>\n\n<h3>1. What banks do and why they exist<\/h3>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Intermediation<\/b>: gather many small deposits, lend in large chunks. Depositors get safety and liquidity; borrowers get credit.<\/li>\n<li><b>Maturity transformation<\/b>: deposits are short-term (you can withdraw anytime), loans are long-term (a home loan for 20 years). This earns a spread but creates liquidity risk.<\/li>\n<li><b>Payments<\/b>: moving money between accounts (UPI, NEFT, RTGS, cards).<\/li>\n<li><b>Risk management<\/b>: assessing borrowers and spreading risk across thousands of loans.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<div class=\"why\"><b>Fractional reserve banking.<\/b> A bank does not keep all deposits in the vault. It keeps a fraction as reserves and lends the rest, which is deposited again elsewhere, and so on. If the reserve fraction is 10%, ₹100 of new deposits can support up to ₹100 ÷ 0.10 = ₹1,000 of total deposits across the system (the money multiplier = 1 ÷ reserve ratio). This is why a run on deposits can sink a bank: it cannot pay everyone at once.<\/div>\n\n<h3>2. A bank's balance sheet (the opposite of a normal firm's)<\/h3>\n<table>\n<tr><th>Assets (uses of funds)<\/th><th>Liabilities and equity (sources)<\/th><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Loans and advances (biggest, earns interest)<\/td><td>Deposits (current, savings, fixed) — owed to customers<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Investments (government bonds, held for SLR)<\/td><td>Borrowings (from other banks, bonds, RBI)<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Cash and balance with RBI (CRR)<\/td><td>Equity capital and reserves<\/td><\/tr>\n<\/table>\n<p>A customer's deposit is a <b>liability<\/b> to the bank because the bank owes it back. A loan the bank gives is an <b>asset<\/b>. A bank is typically leveraged around 10 to 15 times, so a small loss on assets can wipe out its equity. This is the reason for capital rules.<\/p>\n\n<h3>3. How a bank makes money<\/h3>\n<div class=\"fx\">Net interest income (NII) = Interest earned &minus; Interest paid<br>Net interest margin (NIM) = NII &divide; Average earning assets<br>Total income = NII + Other (fee) income<br>Cost-to-income ratio = Operating expenses &divide; Total income<br>Pre-provision profit = Total income &minus; Operating expenses<br>Profit before tax = Pre-provision profit &minus; Provisions<\/div>\n<div class=\"eg\">Interest earned ₹900 crore, interest paid ₹520 crore, average earning assets ₹10,000 crore, fee income ₹120 crore, operating costs ₹250 crore, provisions ₹80 crore.\n<ul><li>NII = 380; NIM = 3.8%.<\/li><li>Total income = 500; cost-to-income = 50%.<\/li><li>Pre-provision profit = 250; PBT = 170.<\/li><\/ul><\/div>\n<p>Banks also earn from fees: loan processing, cards, forex, distributing insurance and mutual funds. Treasury gains on bond trading add volatility.<\/p>\n\n<h3>4. Key performance ratios<\/h3>\n<table>\n<tr><th>Ratio<\/th><th>Formula<\/th><th>What it tells you<\/th><\/tr>\n<tr><td>CASA ratio<\/td><td>(Current + savings deposits) ÷ total deposits<\/td><td>Cheap funding. Higher means lower cost of funds.<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Credit-deposit (CD) ratio<\/td><td>Loans ÷ deposits<\/td><td>How much of deposits is lent out<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Gross NPA ratio<\/td><td>Gross NPAs ÷ gross loans<\/td><td>Share of bad loans<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Net NPA ratio<\/td><td>(Gross NPA − provisions) ÷ (loans − provisions)<\/td><td>Bad loans after cover<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Provision coverage ratio<\/td><td>Provisions ÷ gross NPA<\/td><td>Cushion held against bad loans<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Return on assets (ROA)<\/td><td>Net profit ÷ average assets<\/td><td>1% to 1.5% is good for banks<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Capital adequacy ratio (CAR)<\/td><td>Capital ÷ risk-weighted assets<\/td><td>Loss-absorbing buffer<\/td><\/tr>\n<\/table>\n\n<h3>5. Bad loans: how an NPA forms and what happens<\/h3>\n<ul>\n<li>A loan is a <b>Non-Performing Asset (NPA)<\/b> when interest or principal is overdue for <b>more than 90 days<\/b>.<\/li>\n<li>Early warning: accounts overdue 1 to 30, 31 to 60 and 61 to 90 days are tagged SMA-0, SMA-1 and SMA-2 (special mention accounts).<\/li>\n<li>NPAs are classified as Substandard, Doubtful and Loss. The bank sets aside <b>provisions<\/b> (a percentage of the loan) against them. A provision is an <em>expense<\/em>; it cuts profit even though no cash leaves at that moment.<\/li>\n<li>Recovery routes: restructuring, sale to an asset reconstruction company, SARFAESI action to seize collateral, the insolvency process, or write-off.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<div class=\"eg\">Gross loans ₹50,000 crore; gross NPAs ₹1,500 crore (3.0%); provisions ₹1,050 crore. Provision coverage = 70%. Net NPA = 450 ÷ 48,950 = 0.92%.<\/div>\n\n<h3>6. Capital and risk<\/h3>\n<p>Banks face <b>credit risk<\/b> (borrowers default), <b>liquidity risk<\/b> (can't meet withdrawals), <b>market/interest-rate risk<\/b> (bond prices fall when rates rise; funding costs change faster than loan yields), and <b>operational risk<\/b> (fraud, system failure).<\/p>\n<p><b>Basel III<\/b> sets minimum capital and liquidity. Risk-weighted assets (RWA) give a high weight to risky loans and a low weight to safe assets such as government bonds. Total capital ÷ RWA is the CAR; Indian banks must keep a minimum above the Basel floor of 8% (stems will give the number). <b>Liquidity Coverage Ratio<\/b> requires enough high-quality liquid assets to survive a 30-day stress.<\/p>\n<div class=\"eg\">RWA ₹1,20,000 crore; capital ₹15,600 crore. CAR = 13%. If NPAs force a ₹3,000 crore loss, capital drops to 12,600 and CAR to 10.5%.<\/div>\n\n<h3>7. Regulators and the RBI toolkit<\/h3>\n<table>\n<tr><th>Tool<\/th><th>What it is<\/th><th>Effect<\/th><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Repo rate<\/td><td>Rate at which RBI lends to banks against securities<\/td><td>Raising it makes borrowing costlier, slows credit and inflation; cutting does the opposite<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Reverse repo / SDF<\/td><td>Rate at which RBI absorbs excess money from banks<\/td><td>Floor of the rate corridor<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>MSF<\/td><td>Emergency overnight borrowing, above repo<\/td><td>Ceiling of the corridor<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>CRR<\/td><td>Share of deposits kept as cash with RBI; earns no interest<\/td><td>Higher CRR drains lendable money<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>SLR<\/td><td>Share of deposits kept in government securities, cash or gold<\/td><td>Higher SLR reduces lending capacity<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Open market operations<\/td><td>RBI buys or sells government bonds<\/td><td>Buying injects money, selling absorbs it<\/td><\/tr>\n<\/table>\n<p>Most new floating-rate loans are linked to an external benchmark (for example the repo rate), so changes in the repo rate reach borrowers quickly. Other regulators: <b>SEBI<\/b> (securities markets, mutual funds), <b>IRDAI<\/b> (insurance), <b>PFRDA<\/b> (pensions).<\/p>\n<div class=\"eg\">Deposits ₹2,00,000 crore, CRR 4%, SLR 18%. Cash with RBI = 8,000; government securities = 36,000. Remaining 78% = ₹1,56,000 crore is free for loans and other uses.<\/div>\n\n<h3>8. Types of financial institutions<\/h3>\n<table>\n<tr><th>Institution<\/th><th>Role and key rule<\/th><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Commercial banks (public, private, foreign)<\/td><td>Full banking: deposits, loans, payments<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Small finance banks<\/td><td>Banks focused on small borrowers, with lending targets for priority sectors<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Payments banks<\/td><td>Accept deposits and offer payments but cannot lend<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Co-operative banks<\/td><td>Owned by members; urban and rural<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>NBFCs<\/td><td>Lend and invest (vehicle loans, gold loans, microfinance) but cannot accept demand deposits or issue cheques<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Insurance companies<\/td><td>Pool risk, invest premiums<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Mutual funds / AMCs<\/td><td>Pool investors' money into portfolios<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Stock exchanges, depositories, clearing corporations<\/td><td>Trading, holding of securities in electronic form, settlement<\/td><\/tr>\n<\/table>\n<p><b>Deposit insurance (DICGC)<\/b> covers up to ₹5 lakh per depositor per bank (principal plus interest). Savings above that are exposed if a bank fails.<\/p>\n\n<h3>9. Payments in one glance<\/h3>\n<table>\n<tr><th>System<\/th><th>Use<\/th><\/tr>\n<tr><td>UPI<\/td><td>Instant, 24×7 mobile payments; mostly free for person-to-person<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>IMPS<\/td><td>Instant bank transfers, 24×7<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>NEFT<\/td><td>Batch settlement, no minimum<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>RTGS<\/td><td>Large-value real-time transfers; minimum ₹2 lakh<\/td><\/tr>\n<\/table>\n\n<h3>Common traps<\/h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Treating deposits as a bank's own money or an asset. For the bank they are a liability.<\/li>\n<li>Confusing CRR (cash with RBI, no return) with SLR (securities held by the bank, small return).<\/li>\n<li>Reading a rate cut as making loans dearer.<\/li>\n<li>Forgetting that provisions reduce profit when computing PBT.<\/li>\n<li>Using a bank's ROA benchmark (about 1%) against a normal company's.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n"}, {"id": "bf-val", "sec": "BF", "title": "Valuation", "r1": "Valuation", "min": 45, "html": "\n<p class=\"lead\">Valuation tries to answer \"what is this business, or this project, worth today?\" The two core ideas: a rupee in the future is worth less than a rupee today, and a business is worth the cash it will produce for its owners. Everything else is technique.<\/p>\n\n<h3>1. Value is the present value of future cash<\/h3>\n<div class=\"fx\">Present value = Cash flow &divide; (1 + r)<sup>n<\/sup><\/div>\n<p>If you require 10% a year, ₹110 one year from now is worth 110 ÷ 1.10 = ₹100 today. Discounting is just compounding in reverse. The rate you use (the <b>discount rate<\/b>) reflects the risk: safer cash flows get a lower rate and a higher value.<\/p>\n<div class=\"eg\">A business will pay out ₹100, ₹120 and ₹140 in years 1, 2, 3. At 10%: 100/1.1 + 120/1.21 + 140/1.331 = 90.9 + 99.2 + 105.2 = ₹295.3 today.<\/div>\n\n<h3>2. Project valuation: capital budgeting<\/h3>\n<p>Should we spend ₹X now for cash flows later? Four tools:<\/p>\n<table>\n<tr><th>Method<\/th><th>Rule<\/th><th>Strength<\/th><th>Weakness<\/th><\/tr>\n<tr><td><b>NPV<\/b> = PV of inflows − initial outlay<\/td><td>Accept if NPV &gt; 0<\/td><td>Measures value created in rupees; uses time value<\/td><td>Needs a discount rate<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td><b>IRR<\/b> = rate where NPV = 0<\/td><td>Accept if IRR &gt; required return<\/td><td>Intuitive percentage<\/td><td>Can mislead when ranking projects of different size, or with multiple sign changes<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td><b>Payback<\/b> = years to recover outlay<\/td><td>Accept if under a limit<\/td><td>Simple, shows liquidity risk<\/td><td>Ignores time value and cash after payback<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td><b>Discounted payback<\/b><\/td><td>Same, using discounted flows<\/td><td>Adds time value<\/td><td>Still ignores later cash<\/td><\/tr>\n<\/table>\n<div class=\"eg\"><b>Machine.<\/b> Cost ₹10 lakh now; inflows ₹4 lakh a year for 4 years; required return 12%.\n<ul><li>PV of inflows = 4 × [1 − 1.12<sup>−4<\/sup>] ÷ 0.12 = 4 × 3.0373 = ₹12.15 lakh.<\/li>\n<li>NPV = 12.15 − 10 = <b>+₹2.15 lakh<\/b>, so accept.<\/li>\n<li>Payback = 10 ÷ 4 = 2.5 years.<\/li>\n<li>IRR: about 21.9% (the rate at which the annuity factor equals 10 ÷ 4 = 2.5).<\/li><\/ul>\nWhen projects compete for limited funds, rank by NPV (or profitability index = PV of inflows ÷ outlay). For projects of unequal life, compare equivalent annual cost.<\/div>\n<p><b>Which cash flows count?<\/b> Incremental, after-tax cash flows: add back depreciation (non-cash) but include the tax shield it creates; include working-capital changes; ignore sunk costs and financing costs (these are captured in the discount rate); include opportunity costs.<\/p>\n\n<h3>3. Cost of capital and WACC<\/h3>\n<p>The discount rate for a whole business is its <b>weighted average cost of capital<\/b>: what it pays, on average, to the people who fund it.<\/p>\n<div class=\"fx\">WACC = (E &divide; V) &times; Re + (D &divide; V) &times; Rd &times; (1 &minus; t)<\/div>\n<ul>\n<li><b>E, D<\/b> are the market values of equity and debt; V = E + D.<\/li>\n<li><b>Rd<\/b> is borrowing cost; interest is tax-deductible so the after-tax cost is Rd × (1 − t).<\/li>\n<li><b>Re<\/b> (cost of equity) by CAPM: risk-free rate + beta × equity risk premium. Beta measures how much the stock moves versus the market (beta 1.2: 20% more than the market).<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<div class=\"eg\">Equity ₹510 crore at 15%, debt ₹440 crore at 8% pre-tax, tax 25%. Weights: 53.7% and 46.3%. Rd after tax = 6%. WACC = 0.537 × 15% + 0.463 × 6% = 8.05% + 2.78% = <b>10.83%<\/b>.<\/div>\n\n<h3>4. Capital structure and leverage<\/h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Debt is cheaper than equity (lenders have first claim and interest is tax-deductible) but adds a fixed obligation. More debt raises the risk to owners.<\/li>\n<li><b>Financial leverage<\/b> amplifies EPS changes: Degree of financial leverage = EBIT ÷ (EBIT − interest).<\/li>\n<li>The best structure balances the tax saving against the cost of distress. Too much debt raises the cost of both debt and equity.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<div class=\"eg\">EBIT ₹100, interest ₹20, 10 shares, tax 25%. EPS = (100 − 20) × 0.75 ÷ 10 = ₹6. If EBIT rises 20% to 120, EPS = 100 × 0.75 ÷ 10 = ₹7.5, a 25% rise. DFL = 100 ÷ 80 = 1.25 ✓.<\/div>\n\n<h3>5. Valuing a whole business: DCF<\/h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Forecast free cash flow to the firm (FCFF) for 5 to 10 years: EBIT × (1 − t) + depreciation − capex − increase in working capital.<\/li>\n<li>Choose a discount rate: WACC.<\/li>\n<li>Estimate a <b>terminal value<\/b> for all years after the forecast, using the Gordon growth formula: TV = FCF<sub>n+1<\/sub> ÷ (WACC − g). The growth rate g must be below WACC and below long-run economic growth.<\/li>\n<li>Discount each year's cash flow and the terminal value to today. The total is <b>enterprise value (EV)<\/b>.<\/li>\n<li>Equity value = EV − net debt. Per share = equity value ÷ number of shares.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<div class=\"eg\">FCF next year ₹100 crore, growing 5% forever, WACC 11%. EV = 100 ÷ (0.11 − 0.05) = ₹1,667 crore. Net debt ₹400 crore → equity ₹1,267 crore. With 10 crore shares: ₹126.7 per share.<\/div>\n<div class=\"why\"><b>Sensitivity.<\/b> The terminal value is usually 60% to 80% of a DCF. Change WACC from 11% to 10% in the example and EV becomes 100 ÷ 0.05 = ₹2,000 crore, up 20%. Always show a range, not one number.<\/div>\n\n<h3>6. Enterprise value vs equity value<\/h3>\n<div class=\"fx\">Equity value (market cap) = Share price &times; Shares<br>Enterprise value = Equity value + Debt + Preference + Minority interest &minus; Cash<br>Equity value = EV &minus; Net debt<\/div>\n<p>EV is what it would cost to buy the whole operating business and pay off its debts, net of the cash you acquire. Operating profit metrics (EBITDA, EBIT, sales) belong to <em>all<\/em> capital providers, so they pair with EV. Earnings and book value belong to <em>owners<\/em>, so they pair with equity value.<\/p>\n\n<h3>7. Relative valuation: using multiples<\/h3>\n<table>\n<tr><th>Multiple<\/th><th>Formula<\/th><th>Best for<\/th><\/tr>\n<tr><td>P/E<\/td><td>Price ÷ EPS (or market cap ÷ PAT)<\/td><td>Stable, profit-making firms<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>EV/EBITDA<\/td><td>EV ÷ EBITDA<\/td><td>Capital-heavy firms; compares firms with different debt<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>EV/Sales<\/td><td>EV ÷ revenue<\/td><td>Loss-making or high-growth firms<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>P/B<\/td><td>Price ÷ book value per share<\/td><td>Banks and asset-heavy firms<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>PEG<\/td><td>P/E ÷ growth rate (%)<\/td><td>Adjusting P/E for growth<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Dividend yield<\/td><td>Dividend per share ÷ price<\/td><td>Income stocks<\/td><\/tr>\n<\/table>\n<p>Method: choose comparable companies, compute their multiple, take the median, apply it to your firm's metric. EV/EBITDA of 10 on EBITDA ₹200 crore gives EV ₹2,000 crore. Less net debt ₹500 crore leaves equity ₹1,500 crore. A multiple reflects growth, risk and returns, so firms are comparable only if those are similar.<\/p>\n\n<h3>8. Dividend discount model and CAPM<\/h3>\n<div class=\"fx\">Price = D<sub>1<\/sub> &divide; (r &minus; g) &nbsp;|&nbsp; Re = Rf + &beta; &times; (Rm &minus; Rf)<\/div>\n<p>A stock paying ₹10 next year, growing 4% forever, with a required return of 12%, is worth 10 ÷ 0.08 = ₹125. If the risk-free rate is 7%, market return 12% and beta 1.2, Re = 7% + 1.2 × 5% = 13%.<\/p>\n\n<h3>9. Which method when?<\/h3>\n<table>\n<tr><th>Situation<\/th><th>Best tool<\/th><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Deciding to buy a machine or open a store<\/td><td>NPV / IRR<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Steady, profitable listed firm<\/td><td>P/E, DCF<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Loss-making start-up<\/td><td>EV/Sales, DCF with long forecast<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Bank<\/td><td>P/B with ROE, dividend discount<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Firms with very different debt levels<\/td><td>EV-based multiples<\/td><\/tr>\n<\/table>\n\n<h3>Common traps<\/h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Subtracting net debt twice, or adding it to an EV that is already net of cash.<\/li>\n<li>Discounting FCFF at the cost of equity (use WACC), or FCFE at WACC.<\/li>\n<li>Using the pre-tax cost of debt in WACC.<\/li>\n<li>g greater than or equal to the discount rate, which makes the Gordon formula meaningless.<\/li>\n<li>Counting depreciation as a cash flow rather than adding it back, or ignoring the tax shield.<\/li>\n<li>Including sunk costs or financing costs in project cash flows.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n"}, {"id": "pf-tvm", "sec": "PF", "title": "Time value of money and compounding", "r1": "Time value of money and compounding", "min": 30, "html": "\n<p class=\"lead\">Money you have today can be invested and grow, so a rupee today is worth more than a rupee later. This one idea powers every loan, SIP, retirement plan and inflation calculation in personal finance.<\/p>\n<h3>1. Simple vs compound interest<\/h3>\n<p><b>Simple interest<\/b> is earned only on the original amount: SI = P × r × t. ₹1,00,000 at 8% for 5 years earns 8,000 a year, total ₹40,000, ending at ₹1,40,000.<\/p>\n<p><b>Compound interest<\/b> is earned on the original amount <em>and on earlier interest<\/em>. Each year's interest is added and starts earning too.<\/p>\n<div class=\"fx\">FV = PV &times; (1 + r)<sup>n<\/sup> &nbsp;|&nbsp; PV = FV &divide; (1 + r)<sup>n<\/sup><\/div>\n<p>The same ₹1,00,000 at 8% compounded yearly for 5 years becomes 1,00,000 × 1.08<sup>5<\/sup> = ₹1,46,933. The extra ₹6,933 is interest on interest. Over longer periods the gap becomes enormous, which is why starting early matters more than investing more.<\/p>\n<h3>2. Compounding frequency and effective rate<\/h3>\n<p>If interest is added more than once a year, you earn slightly more. A \"12% nominal rate compounded monthly\" means 1% each month.<\/p>\n<div class=\"fx\">EAR = (1 + r/m)<sup>m<\/sup> &minus; 1<\/div>\n<p>EAR for 12% monthly = 1.01<sup>12<\/sup> − 1 = 12.68%. Quarterly gives 12.55%. When comparing deposits or loans, compare effective rates, not nominal.<\/p>\n<h3>3. Rules of thumb<\/h3>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Rule of 72<\/b>: years to double ≈ 72 ÷ rate%. At 8%, about 9 years; at 12%, 6 years; at 6% inflation, prices double in 12 years.<\/li>\n<li>Tripling ≈ 114 ÷ rate; quadrupling ≈ 144 ÷ rate.<\/li>\n<li>Starting early beats investing more later: ₹5,000 a month at 12% for 20 years grows to about ₹49.5 lakh, while the same amount for only the last 10 years grows to about ₹11.5 lakh.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h3>4. Regular investing and loans: annuities<\/h3>\n<p>An <b>annuity<\/b> is a series of equal payments at regular intervals (a SIP, an EMI, a pension).<\/p>\n<div class=\"fx\">FV of annuity = P &times; [(1 + r)<sup>n<\/sup> &minus; 1] &divide; r<br>PV of annuity = P &times; [1 &minus; (1 + r)<sup>&minus;n<\/sup>] &divide; r<br>Perpetuity (forever): PV = P &divide; r<\/div>\n<p>Use the monthly rate and number of months for monthly payments. Payments at the <em>start<\/em> of each period (annuity due) are worth (1 + r) times the ordinary annuity.<\/p>\n<div class=\"eg\">₹10,000 a month for 15 years at 12% a year (1% a month): FV = 10,000 × (1.01<sup>180<\/sup> − 1) ÷ 0.01 = 10,000 × 499.58 = <b>₹49.96 lakh<\/b>. You paid in only ₹18 lakh; the rest is growth.<\/div>\n<h3>5. Inflation and real returns<\/h3>\n<p>Prices rise, so a rupee buys less in future. The <b>real return<\/b> is what your money gains in purchasing power.<\/p>\n<div class=\"fx\">Real return = (1 + nominal) &divide; (1 + inflation) &minus; 1<\/div>\n<p>10% nominal with 6% inflation: 1.10 ÷ 1.06 − 1 = 3.77% (not 4%). A goal in the future must be inflated: ₹20 lakh today at 8% education inflation costs 20 × 1.08<sup>10<\/sup> = ₹43.2 lakh in 10 years.<\/p>\n<h3>6. CAGR<\/h3>\n<div class=\"fx\">CAGR = (End value &divide; Start value)<sup>1/years<\/sup> &minus; 1<\/div>\n<p>₹2 lakh growing to ₹5 lakh in 8 years: 2.5<sup>1/8<\/sup> − 1 = 12.1% a year. CAGR smooths a bumpy path into one steady rate.<\/p>\n<h3>7. Moving money in time<\/h3>\n<p>You can only add or compare rupees that sit at the same date. To compare ₹50,000 now with ₹60,000 in 2 years at 8%: bring both to today (60,000 ÷ 1.08<sup>2<\/sup> = ₹51,440). The later sum is worth slightly more.<\/p>\n<div class=\"trap\"><b>Common traps.<\/b> Using the annual rate with monthly periods. Mixing simple and compound interest. Quoting the nominal rate as the effective rate. Adding rupees from different years. Forgetting to inflate a future goal.<\/div>\n"}, {"id": "pf-tax", "sec": "PF", "title": "Income, tax and budgeting", "r1": "Income, tax and budgeting", "min": 35, "html": "\n<p class=\"lead\">Income tax and budgets decide how much of what you earn you actually keep and can save. The tax rules change often, so exam questions state them. Your job is to apply the method correctly.<\/p>\n<h3>1. From gross salary to take-home<\/h3>\n<ul>\n<li><b>CTC<\/b> (cost to company) includes items you never see in hand, such as employer PF and gratuity.<\/li>\n<li><b>Gross salary<\/b> = basic + HRA + allowances + bonuses. <b>Net (take-home)<\/b> = gross − income tax (TDS) − employee PF − other deductions.<\/li>\n<li>Employer and employee PF contributions are usually 12% of basic each (the stem will say).<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h3>2. How income tax is calculated<\/h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Add up all income (salary, interest, rent, capital gains).<\/li>\n<li>Subtract the <b>standard deduction<\/b>, exemptions and any allowed deductions to get <b>taxable income<\/b>.<\/li>\n<li>Apply <b>progressive slabs<\/b>: each slab's rate applies only to the income inside that slab.<\/li>\n<li>Subtract any <b>rebate<\/b> (it can make tax zero below a threshold).<\/li>\n<li>Add <b>cess<\/b> (a percentage of the tax, not of income).<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<div class=\"eg\"><b>Slabs as given in a stem:<\/b> nil up to ₹4 lakh, 5% from 4 to 8 lakh, 10% from 8 to 12 lakh. Taxable income ₹10 lakh.\n<ul><li>0 to 4 lakh: 0<\/li><li>4 to 8 lakh: 4,00,000 × 5% = 20,000<\/li><li>8 to 10 lakh: 2,00,000 × 10% = 20,000<\/li><li>Tax = 40,000; cess at 4% = 1,600; total ₹41,600.<\/li><\/ul>\nAverage rate = 41,600 ÷ 10,00,000 = 4.16%. Marginal rate = 10% (the next rupee is taxed at 10%).<\/div>\n<div class=\"why\"><b>Marginal vs average.<\/b> Progressive tax means a raise never reduces take-home pay: only the extra income is taxed at the higher rate, not all of it. Applying the top rate to the whole income is the most common error.<\/div>\n<h3>3. Deductions vs rebates<\/h3>\n<p>A <b>deduction<\/b> reduces taxable income (80C investments up to ₹1.5 lakh, health insurance under 80D, home-loan interest, in the regime that allows them). A <b>rebate or credit<\/b> reduces the tax itself. ₹1 lakh of deduction at a 30% slab saves ₹30,000; ₹1 lakh of tax credit saves ₹1 lakh. Two regimes may exist side by side (old with deductions, new with lower rates and few deductions); to choose, compute tax both ways.<\/p>\n<h3>4. HRA exemption<\/h3>\n<p>The tax-free part of HRA is the <b>least<\/b> of: (a) HRA received; (b) rent paid − 10% of basic; (c) 50% of basic in metros (40% elsewhere). The rest is taxable.<\/p>\n<div class=\"eg\">Basic ₹8,40,000; HRA received ₹3,94,430; rent paid ₹2,86,763; non-metro (40%).\n(a) 3,94,430; (b) 2,86,763 − 84,000 = 2,02,763; (c) 3,36,000. Least = <b>₹2,02,763<\/b>.<\/div>\n<h3>5. Capital gains<\/h3>\n<p>Gain = sale price − purchase price − costs. Gains held long are usually taxed at a different (often lower) rate than short-term gains, and equity often has an annual exempt amount. Tax applies to the <b>gain<\/b>, not the sale value. Rates come from the stem.<\/p>\n<h3>6. Budgeting from first principles<\/h3>\n<p>A budget is a plan for every rupee that arrives. The aim is to make saving automatic and spending intentional.<\/p>\n<table>\n<tr><th>Method<\/th><th>How it works<\/th><\/tr>\n<tr><td>50/30/20<\/td><td>50% needs, 30% wants, 20% savings and debt repayment<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Pay yourself first<\/td><td>Move savings out on payday; spend what is left<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Zero-based<\/td><td>Assign every rupee a job; income − allocations = 0<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Category caps<\/td><td>Fixed limit per category (food, travel)<\/td><\/tr>\n<\/table>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Savings rate<\/b> = savings ÷ income. 20% or more is healthy.<\/li>\n<li><b>Fixed-obligation ratio<\/b> = EMIs and fixed bills ÷ net income; keep under about 40%.<\/li>\n<li><b>Emergency fund<\/b> = about 6 months of essential expenses (9 to 12 for irregular income). Keep it in liquid, safe instruments.<\/li>\n<li>Separate <b>needs<\/b> (rent, groceries, insurance, EMIs) from <b>wants<\/b> (dining out, subscriptions). Irregular costs (insurance premiums, festivals) go into a monthly <b>sinking fund<\/b>.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<div class=\"trap\"><b>Common traps.<\/b> Applying the top slab to the whole income. Forgetting cess, or applying it to income instead of tax. Subtracting deductions from the tax instead of from income. Counting a one-time bonus as monthly income in a budget.<\/div>\n"}, {"id": "pf-debt", "sec": "PF", "title": "Debt and credit", "r1": "Debt and credit", "min": 35, "html": "\n<p class=\"lead\">Debt lets you spend now and pay later, at a price. It is a useful tool when it buys something that earns or saves more than it costs (a home, an education) and a trap when it funds consumption at a high rate. The skill is finding the true cost.<\/p>\n<h3>1. How a loan works<\/h3>\n<p>You borrow a principal. Each month you pay an <b>EMI<\/b> (equated monthly instalment), a fixed amount that covers that month's interest and some principal. Interest each month = outstanding balance × monthly rate, so early EMIs are mostly interest and later ones mostly principal.<\/p>\n<div class=\"fx\">EMI = P &times; r &times; (1 + r)<sup>n<\/sup> &divide; [(1 + r)<sup>n<\/sup> &minus; 1]<br>(r = monthly rate, n = number of months) &nbsp; In a sheet: =-PMT(rate/12, years*12, loan)<\/div>\n<div class=\"eg\">₹10 lakh at 10% for 5 years: r = 0.8333%, n = 60. EMI = ₹21,247. Total paid = ₹12.75 lakh; interest = ₹2.75 lakh. First month: interest 8,333, principal 12,914.\nSame loan over 10 years: EMI ₹13,215, total interest ₹5.86 lakh. A longer tenure lowers the EMI but more than doubles the interest.<\/div>\n<h3>2. Reducing balance vs flat rate<\/h3>\n<p>A <b>flat rate<\/b> charges interest on the original loan for the whole term even though you keep repaying. 8% flat for 3 years looks cheap but costs about 14.5% a year on a reducing balance. Always convert to an effective rate before comparing.<\/p>\n<h3>3. Prepayment<\/h3>\n<p>Paying principal early saves interest on that money for the rest of the loan. Early prepayment saves far more than late. When you prepay, choose to <b>reduce the tenure<\/b> (saves more) or reduce the EMI (eases cash flow).<\/p>\n<h3>4. Credit cards<\/h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Pay the <b>full statement balance<\/b> by the due date and you pay no interest (the interest-free period can reach about 45 days).<\/li>\n<li>Carrying a balance costs about 36% to 42% a year; paying only the minimum keeps you in debt for years.<\/li>\n<li>Cash withdrawals attract interest from day one plus a fee.<\/li>\n<li>\"No-cost EMI\" usually has the cost hidden in a lost discount or fees.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h3>5. Credit score<\/h3>\n<p>A score (300 to 900 in India; 750 and above is good) summarises your repayment record. It sets whether you are approved and the interest rate you are offered, which can mean lakhs saved on a home loan.<\/p>\n<table>\n<tr><th>Factor<\/th><th>What helps<\/th><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Payment history (largest)<\/td><td>Never miss a due date<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Credit utilisation<\/td><td>Use below about 30% of your limit<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Credit age<\/td><td>Keep old accounts open<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Mix<\/td><td>Both secured and unsecured, in moderation<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Enquiries<\/td><td>Avoid many applications in a short time<\/td><\/tr>\n<\/table>\n<h3>6. Repayment strategies<\/h3>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Avalanche<\/b>: pay the minimum on all, put every spare rupee on the highest-interest debt. Lowest total cost.<\/li>\n<li><b>Snowball<\/b>: clear the smallest balance first. Costs more but gives early wins.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<div class=\"eg\">Debts: card ₹2 lakh at 36%, personal loan ₹5 lakh at 14%, home loan ₹20 lakh at 9%. Every ₹20,000 paid off on the card saves ₹7,200 a year; on the home loan only ₹1,800. Clear the card first.<\/div>\n<h3>7. Home loans<\/h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Lenders cap the loan at a share of the property value and look for EMIs under about 40% to 50% of net income.<\/li>\n<li>Floating rates move with the benchmark; when rates rise, the usual effect is a longer tenure at the same EMI.<\/li>\n<li>Rent vs buy: compare the total cost of owning (EMI, maintenance, tax, opportunity cost of the down payment) with rent plus the returns on the money not tied up.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<div class=\"trap\"><b>Common traps.<\/b> Comparing EMIs instead of total cost. Using annual rate and years in the EMI formula. Treating the minimum due as a repayment plan. Taking a long tenure and thinking the loan is \"cheaper\".<\/div>\n"}, {"id": "pf-ins", "sec": "PF", "title": "Insurance and risk", "r1": "Insurance and risk", "min": 30, "html": "\n<p class=\"lead\">Insurance moves a risk you cannot afford to bear to a company that pools many people's risks, in exchange for a small, known premium. It is protection, not an investment.<\/p>\n<h3>1. How insurance works<\/h3>\n<p>Thousands of people each pay a small premium. Only a few suffer a loss, and the pool pays them. The insurer prices premiums so that total premiums cover expected claims plus costs. For you, a small certain cost replaces a small chance of a ruinous loss.<\/p>\n<h3>2. Words you must know<\/h3>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Premium<\/b>: what you pay. <b>Sum assured / sum insured<\/b>: the cover.<\/li>\n<li><b>Deductible<\/b>: you pay the first part of each claim. <b>Co-pay<\/b>: you pay a fixed share of each claim.<\/li>\n<li><b>Waiting period<\/b>: time before some illnesses are covered. <b>Pre-existing disease<\/b> must be disclosed.<\/li>\n<li><b>Moral hazard<\/b>: insured people take more risk. <b>Adverse selection<\/b>: those most likely to claim buy the most cover.<\/li>\n<li><b>Insurable interest<\/b>: you must lose financially if the event happens.<\/li>\n<li><b>Underinsurance<\/b>: if you insure only part of the value, partial claims can be cut in proportion (average clause).<\/li>\n<li><b>Claim settlement ratio<\/b>: share of claims paid. Check the amount too.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h3>3. Products<\/h3>\n<table>\n<tr><th>Product<\/th><th>Pays when<\/th><th>Notes<\/th><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Term life<\/td><td>You die in the term<\/td><td>Pure cover, cheapest per rupee; buy early<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Health<\/td><td>Hospital costs<\/td><td>Check room-rent limits, co-pay, sub-limits, waiting periods; add a super top-up<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Critical illness / accident<\/td><td>Lump sum on listed event<\/td><td>Supplements health cover<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Motor<\/td><td>Damage or liability<\/td><td>Third-party cover is compulsory<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Home<\/td><td>Fire, theft, calamity<\/td><td>Insure rebuild cost, not land value<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Endowment, ULIP, money-back<\/td><td>Death or maturity<\/td><td>Mix insurance and investment; usually costly with low cover<\/td><\/tr>\n<\/table>\n<h3>4. How much life cover?<\/h3>\n<p>Rule of thumb: 10 to 15 times annual income. Needs-based: PV of dependants' expenses + loans + goals − existing assets and cover.<\/p>\n<div class=\"eg\">Family needs ₹6 lakh a year for 25 years; discount at a 6% real return. PV = 6,00,000 × [1 − 1.06<sup>−25<\/sup>] ÷ 0.06 = ₹76.7 lakh. Add loans ₹30 lakh, subtract assets ₹20 lakh and existing cover ₹10 lakh. Needed ≈ ₹76.7 lakh.<\/div>\n<h3>5. Managing risk<\/h3>\n<ol><li><b>Avoid<\/b> the risky activity.<\/li><li><b>Reduce<\/b> likelihood or size (seat belts, fitness).<\/li><li><b>Retain<\/b> small risks from your emergency fund.<\/li><li><b>Transfer<\/b> large, unlikely, severe risks through insurance.<\/li><\/ol>\n<h3>6. Insurance vs investment<\/h3>\n<p>A traditional plan with a ₹5 lakh premium may give ₹50 lakh cover and a low return. A term plan for ₹12,000 gives ₹1 crore of cover, and investing the ₹4.88 lakh difference in an index fund compounds far better. For pure protection, buy term and invest separately. Do not insure a child's life for income replacement when no one depends on the child's income.<\/p>\n<div class=\"trap\"><b>Common traps.<\/b> Confusing sum assured with the return you will get. Choosing a plan on premium alone. Ignoring medical inflation. Not disclosing pre-existing conditions (claims can be rejected).<\/div>\n"}, {"id": "pf-inv", "sec": "PF", "title": "Investments and investment behaviour", "r1": "Investments and investment behaviour", "min": 40, "html": "\n<p class=\"lead\">Investing is giving up money today for a larger amount later, accepting some risk. Good investing is mostly two skills: matching assets to your time horizon, and not letting your own psychology wreck the plan.<\/p>\n<h3>1. Return, risk, and time<\/h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Higher expected return comes with higher risk. There is no reliable free lunch.<\/li>\n<li><b>Risk<\/b> is the chance of a worse outcome and wider swings, measured by standard deviation. <b>Sharpe ratio<\/b> = (return − risk-free rate) ÷ standard deviation: return per unit of risk.<\/li>\n<li>Longer horizons let you take more risk: equity for 7+ years, debt for 1 to 3 years, savings or liquid funds under 1 year.<\/li>\n<li><b>Diversification<\/b> removes company-specific risk but not market-wide risk.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h3>2. Asset classes<\/h3>\n<table>\n<tr><th>Asset<\/th><th>Return from<\/th><th>Risk<\/th><th>Liquidity<\/th><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Savings, liquid funds<\/td><td>Interest<\/td><td>Very low<\/td><td>High<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Fixed deposits, bonds<\/td><td>Interest<\/td><td>Low to medium<\/td><td>Medium<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Equity / equity funds<\/td><td>Earnings growth, dividends<\/td><td>High short term<\/td><td>High<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Gold<\/td><td>Price change<\/td><td>Medium<\/td><td>High<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Real estate<\/td><td>Rent + price<\/td><td>Medium<\/td><td>Low<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>PPF, EPF, NPS<\/td><td>Government-linked, tax benefits<\/td><td>Low<\/td><td>Locked in<\/td><\/tr>\n<\/table>\n<h3>3. Bonds<\/h3>\n<p>A bond is a loan to a government or company: you receive fixed coupon payments and the face value at maturity. Bond <b>prices fall when interest rates rise<\/b> (new bonds pay more, so old ones are worth less), and longer bonds fall more. <b>Yield to maturity<\/b> is the return if held to the end. Credit rating shows default risk; lower rating means higher yield.<\/p>\n<h3>4. Mutual funds<\/h3>\n<ul>\n<li>A fund pools money. <b>NAV<\/b> = (assets − liabilities) ÷ units.<\/li>\n<li><b>Expense ratio<\/b> is deducted every year; direct plans cost less than regular plans. A 1% annual gap can cost about a fifth of the final corpus over 20 years.<\/li>\n<li><b>Index funds<\/b> copy an index at low cost; most active funds fail to beat the index after fees over long periods.<\/li>\n<li><b>SIP<\/b>: fixed amount at regular intervals. It averages your purchase price (rupee-cost averaging) and builds discipline. It does not guarantee profit.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h3>5. Portfolio return and risk<\/h3>\n<p>Portfolio return = weighted average of asset returns. Example: 60% equity at 12% and 40% debt at 7% gives 0.6 × 12 + 0.4 × 7 = 10%. Risk is not a simple average: assets that do not move together lower the portfolio's risk (diversification). A loss of 20% needs a gain of 25% to recover (1 ÷ 0.8 − 1).<\/p>\n<h3>6. Asset allocation<\/h3>\n<p>Rule of thumb: equity share ≈ 100 − age, adjusted for <b>risk capacity<\/b> (stable income, few dependants) and <b>risk tolerance<\/b> (comfort with falls). <b>Rebalance<\/b> once a year to the target mix; it makes you sell what has risen and buy what has fallen.<\/p>\n<h3>7. Behavioural biases<\/h3>\n<table>\n<tr><th>Bias<\/th><th>What happens<\/th><th>Remedy<\/th><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Loss aversion<\/td><td>A loss hurts about twice as much as an equal gain pleases; leads to panic selling or holding losers<\/td><td>Predefined plan, long horizon<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Recency bias<\/td><td>Recent returns assumed to continue<\/td><td>Look at long-term history<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Herd behaviour / FOMO<\/td><td>Buying what everyone buys<\/td><td>Invest by plan, not by buzz<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Overconfidence<\/td><td>Trading too much, concentrating<\/td><td>Index core; track results honestly<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Anchoring<\/td><td>Fixating on purchase price<\/td><td>Judge on future prospects<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Disposition effect<\/td><td>Selling winners early, holding losers<\/td><td>Exit rules<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Mental accounting<\/td><td>Treating money differently by source<\/td><td>Look at total wealth<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Confirmation bias<\/td><td>Seeking views that agree with you<\/td><td>Seek the opposing case<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Sunk-cost fallacy<\/td><td>Holding because you have already lost<\/td><td>Ask: would I buy it today?<\/td><\/tr>\n<\/table>\n<div class=\"eg\">After a 20% fall an investor sells everything \"to stop losses\". The fund must now rise 25% to recover. By selling, the investor locks in the loss and misses the recovery: loss aversion plus panic.<\/div>\n<h3>8. Fraud red flags<\/h3>\n<p>Guaranteed high returns, pressure to act now, unregistered advisers, schemes that pay you for recruiting others, \"insider tips\", requests for OTPs. Check registration with SEBI before investing.<\/p>\n<div class=\"trap\"><b>Common traps.<\/b> Judging a fund by one year's return. Confusing high past return with high future return. Believing many funds with the same holdings is diversification. Ignoring tax, fees and inflation when comparing products.<\/div>\n"}, {"id": "pf-nw", "sec": "PF", "title": "Net worth and goals", "r1": "Net worth and goals", "min": 30, "html": "\n<p class=\"lead\">Net worth is your financial scoreboard; goals are where you want it to go. Planning is turning a goal with a date and a price into a monthly amount you can act on.<\/p>\n<h3>1. Net worth<\/h3>\n<div class=\"fx\">Net worth = Total assets &minus; Total liabilities<\/div>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Assets<\/b>: bank balances, investments (at current value), property, vehicles, gold, retirement accounts.<\/li>\n<li><b>Liabilities<\/b>: home, car and personal loans, card dues, money owed.<\/li>\n<li>Value assets at what they would sell for today, not what you paid.<\/li>\n<li>Net worth rises by saving and by asset growth; it falls by borrowing for spending and by asset falls.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<div class=\"eg\">Assets: investments ₹8 lakh, cash ₹2 lakh, flat ₹60 lakh, car ₹5 lakh = ₹75 lakh. Liabilities: home loan ₹35 lakh, car loan ₹2 lakh = ₹37 lakh. Net worth = <b>₹38 lakh<\/b>. Liquid assets (investments plus cash) = ₹10 lakh.<\/div>\n<h3>2. Financial health ratios<\/h3>\n<table>\n<tr><th>Ratio<\/th><th>Formula<\/th><th>Healthy<\/th><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Savings rate<\/td><td>Savings ÷ income<\/td><td>20% or more<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Emergency fund cover<\/td><td>Liquid savings ÷ monthly essential expenses<\/td><td>6 months<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Debt-service ratio<\/td><td>Monthly EMIs ÷ net income<\/td><td>Under 40%<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Solvency ratio<\/td><td>Net worth ÷ total assets<\/td><td>Higher is better<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Liquidity ratio<\/td><td>Liquid assets ÷ monthly expenses<\/td><td>3 to 6 months<\/td><\/tr>\n<\/table>\n<h3>3. Cash flow<\/h3>\n<p>Cash flow = income − expenses − EMIs. Positive surplus is what builds net worth. A negative surplus is financed by borrowing or selling assets and shrinks net worth. Plot the month to see where surplus leaks.<\/p>\n<h3>4. Goal-based planning: five steps<\/h3>\n<ol>\n<li><b>Define<\/b> the goal, its price in today's rupees and its date.<\/li>\n<li><b>Inflate<\/b> the cost to the goal date: future cost = today's cost × (1 + inflation)<sup>years<\/sup>.<\/li>\n<li><b>Choose assets<\/b> to fit the horizon (equity for 7+ years, debt for under 3).<\/li>\n<li><b>Compute the monthly investment<\/b>: =-PMT(return/12, months, 0, future_cost).<\/li>\n<li><b>Review yearly<\/b>, step up the SIP with income, and rebalance.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<div class=\"eg\"><b>Education fund.<\/b> ₹20 lakh today, 8% inflation, needed in 10 years: 20 × 1.08<sup>10<\/sup> = ₹43.2 lakh. At a 12% return (1% a month), the 120-month annuity factor is 230.04, so SIP ≈ 43.2 lakh ÷ 230.04 ≈ <b>₹18,800 a month<\/b>.<\/div>\n<div class=\"eg\"><b>Retirement.<\/b> Expenses ₹50,000 a month today, 6% inflation, retire in 25 years. First-year expenses = 6,00,000 × 1.06<sup>25<\/sup> = ₹25.8 lakh. With a safe withdrawal rate of about 4%, the corpus needed is about 25 times annual expenses, roughly ₹6.4 crore. Then work back to a monthly SIP over 25 years.<\/div>\n<h3>5. Prioritising goals<\/h3>\n<p>Order: (1) emergency fund and insurance, (2) clear high-interest debt, (3) retirement saving, (4) medium-term goals, (5) wants. Loans exist for education; none exist for retirement, so do not sacrifice retirement saving to over-fund a child's education.<\/p>\n<p>Use <b>sinking funds<\/b> for known future costs (annual premiums, fees, festivals) and a <b>step-up SIP<\/b> that rises each year with income.<\/p>\n<div class=\"trap\"><b>Common traps.<\/b> Forgetting to inflate the goal. Using a nominal return with a goal stated in today's rupees, or the reverse. Counting the house you live in as a liquid asset. Treating a loan as an asset.<\/div>\n"}];
